% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% CHAT - the chat client, an ordinary SINTRAN PROG.
%
% COMPILED AND RUN ON D100, 2026-08-18. Two terminals, one room, both
% directions:
%
%     @CHAT
%     CHAT: joining, type and press return
%     RONNY: HELLO FROM A REAL ND
%
% This header said "NOT COMPILED, NOT RUN" for as long as that was true and for
% a while after it stopped being true, which is its own small lesson - see the
% note at the end of the file about what else went stale with it.
%
% Run it with @CHAT. It joins CHAT-LOBBY on the local system, then sits in a
% loop watching two things at once: the keyboard and the port. Type a line and
% it is said to the room; anything the room says appears on the screen.
%
% THE TWO-THINGS-AT-ONCE PROBLEM, which looked like the hard part, is solved by
% two facts from the manuals rather than by any trickery:
%
%   XMPFRCV WITHOUT XFWTF returns AT ONCE rather than waiting, so the port can
%   be polled. A message arrived exactly when the status is POSITIVE: the
%   constants file says the message TYPE is the successful status from XFRCV,
%   and the types run XMTNO=1 to XMTPS=6 while every user error is negative.
%
%   TerminalNoWait (MON 307B) switches the terminal to no-wait, after which
%   InByte (MON 1B) returns at once too. Device 1 is your own terminal.
%
% So no second RT program, no shared buffer and no timeouts: an ordinary loop.
%
% AND WHAT DOES InByte GIVE BACK WHEN NOTHING WAS TYPED? Not a character:
% "In No Wait, the program does not wait for input or output to complete.
% Monitor calls like InByte return ERROR CODE 3 instead." - the NoWaitSwitch
% entry in the Monitor Calls manual. In PLANC a monitor-call error arrives as a
% ROUTINEERROR, handled with ON ROUTINEERROR DO ... ENDON and read from ErrCode,
% which is the form that manual's own PLANC example for InByte uses.
%
% An earlier version of this loop assumed InByte returned some value that would
% mask to 31 or below and could be ignored. That was a guess and it was wrong.
%
% THE PARITY TRAP, and the Monitor Calls manual warns about it in so many
% words: terminal input sets bit 7 as an even-parity bit, so carriage return
% arrives as 141, NOT 13. Compare against 13 and the client never sees the user
% press return, and looks hung rather than wrong. Everything read is masked
% with 127 before it is looked at.
%
% Sending the join is the letter sequence from the COSMOS Programmer Guide's
% own worked example (page 99): XMPBLET builds an XSLET letter header in a
% local buffer, XMPFGET reserves an XMSG buffer, XMPFWRI writes the header into
% it and then writes OUR data after it, and XMPROUT sends the whole thing via
% XROUT. The room's name is looked up by XROUT; we never need its address.
% That trailing-data arrangement is exactly how the join carries a nickname.
%
% THREE CORRECTIONS FROM APPENDIX G, the guide's own sample CLIENT and SERVER:
%
%   flags are 2**BIT      XFWTF is a BIT POSITION (15), not a flags word. The
%                         samples both write "2**XFWTF =: FLAGS". Passing 15
%                         would set bits 0,1,2,3 - four unrelated options - and
%                         the wait would simply not happen.
%   XMMSGIDENTIFIER       the message-identifier type, with two M's. Written
%                         "XMMSGIDENTIFIER: MSGIDENT" in the sample.
%   XMUSERADDRESS         and the address idiom is
%                         ADDR(BUF(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS.
%
%                         ROUND brackets. The guide's sample prints this with
%                         square ones and it does not compile: PLANC F answers
%                         EXPECTS ")" ILLEGAL SYNTAX "[" and then INVALID
%                         PARAMETER LIST for the call around it. Array indexing
%                         in this language uses parentheses like everything
%                         else. Trust the compiler over the typesetting.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% THE INCLUDE NAMES ON THIS MACHINE, and they are NOT the manual's.
% The COSMOS guide says XMP:DEFS and XMP:IMPT. Neither exists on D100:
% the constants ship as (UTILITY)XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL, and there is no
% :IMPT file at all - the whole UTILITY user was listed to be sure. So the
% routine and type declarations are ours, built from the guide's reference
% tables and the PLANC manual's declaration syntax.
%
% Our own include is called XMPIMP:INCL on the machine, short, because our
% file transfer cannot carry a filespec longer than 13 characters. The repo
% keeps it under the readable name XMSG-PL-IMPORT.INCL and the build renames
% it on the way over; do not "fix" this to the long name or the transfer
% silently has nothing to send.
MODULE chat

% THE INCLUDES GO INSIDE THE MODULE, and this is not a style choice. Placed
% above MODULE they are compiled into the outer scope, the module body cannot
% see them, and every call fails with NOT PREVIOUSLY DECLARED "XMPFOPN" while
% the include's own lines draw no diagnostic at all - so the declarations look
% accepted and are simply not there. Measured on D100: 33 diagnostics, none of
% them on the include. The COSMOS guide's appendix G sample has them after
% MODULE for exactly this reason.
% THE REAL COSMOS BINDING, installed from ND-10609 on 1998-08-17 (2026-08-17
% our time) by the product's own COS-LIB-IN-B02:PROG. These are the genuine ND
% files, not our transcription:
%
%   XMP-B02:DEFS   the constants, including the ones that exist nowhere else -
%                  XMOK=0, and the XMX... standard status values. Its own
%                  comment records that the library REMAPS the kernel's status:
%                  "NOTE change from XMSG OK=1".
%   XMP-B02:IMPT   the types and the IMPORT declarations for 54 routines.
%   XMP-100-1-B02:BRF  the code behind those names, linked in by CHATCC:MODE.
%
% DO NOT ALSO INCLUDE XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL. The two files share 184 constant
% names, so including both is 184 redefinitions. VALUES-L is the authority for
% the RAW MON 200B interface and is newer than B02; DEFS is the authority for
% this library layer. One file per layer - they do not compete.
$INCLUDE XMP-B02:DEFS
$INCLUDE XMP-B02:IMPT

% ---- THE SCREEN HALF -----------------------------------------------------
%
% EVERY IMPORT MUST BE UP HERE, BEFORE ANY ROUTINE. These arrived with the
% screen renderer and were first spliced in beside it, just above PROGRAM -
% which is after every routine this client already had. The compiler answered
% MISPLACED STATEMENT "IMPORT" twenty-six times, once per line, and not one
% of those messages says WHERE an import is supposed to go.
% --------------------------------------------------------------------------
$INCLUDE screen

    % ---- MONITOR CALLS ---------------------------------------------------
    % MON72 EESCF - give the escape key back to SINTRAN.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : MON72 )
    % MON71 DESCF - take the escape key AWAY from SINTRAN, so ESC arrives here
    % as an ordinary byte instead of aborting the program.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : MON71 )
    % MON3 ECHOM - the echo. Strategy below zero means NO echo.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, BOOLEAN ARRAY PACKED) : MON3 )
    % MON16 MGTIY - the packed CTYTP word. ONLY valid after VTM's first call.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : MON16 )
    % MON1 is NOT imported here - the client already imports it further up.
    % A second IMPORT of the same name is ILLEGAL PREDECLARATION, which is
    % an ERROR and not a warning.
    % MON66 ISIZE - how many bytes are WAITING. This is what makes a poll
    % possible, and with it a timer.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : MON66 )
    % MON11 TIME - basic time units, a fiftieth of a second each.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER4 : MON11 )
    % MN104 HOLD - sleep. The O drops at three digits.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : MN104 )
    % MN113 CLOCK - seven elements. Element 6 is the FULL year, 1998 not 98.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER ARRAY WRITE) : MN113 )

    % ---- VTM -------------------------------------------------------------
    % VTPCUR is (LINE, POSITION) - row first. MEASURED; two vendor programs
    % name the arguments the other way round and the machine disagrees.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : VTPCUR )
    % VTDSCR is the VIEWPORT. It moves VTM's origin, it CLIPS, and it does NOT
    % nest - a second call replaces the first and reads its rectangle against
    % the physical screen.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : VTDSCR )
    % VTCREC clears a rectangle, viewport-relative.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : VTCREC )
    % VTWBUF FLUSHES. No arguments. Without it, anything drawn without a
    % keystroke behind it sits in VTM's buffer and the program looks dead.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : VTWBUF )
    % VTINBT - READ ONE KEY, DECODED BY VTM.
    %
    % THIS IS WHY THE PROGRAM DOES NOT LOOK AT BYTES. A function key is an
    % escape sequence and every terminal sends a different one; decoding it
    % here would mean a table per terminal, which is precisely VTM's job and
    % VTM already has one. Hand-decoding also silently breaks the moment
    % somebody runs this on a Tandberg.
    %
    % Shape from the STAR-TREK sources: IVTINBT(INP, 2, GETCTRL, 3). Three
    % values IN, one result OUT - and getting that typing right is the whole
    % trick. MEASURED 2026-08-25: declared with every argument WRITE, PAGE UP
    % came back as FOUR raw bytes 27 91 53 126 and it looked as though VTM
    % decoded nothing. A PLANC WRITE parameter is passed by ADDRESS, so the
    % by-value arguments were being handed pointers. Typed properly, the same
    % key comes back as ONE call with the code 201.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, &
                                    INTEGER WRITE, INTEGER) : VTINBT )


    % ---- CTYTP BITS ------------------------------------------------------
    % Ask what a terminal can DO, never what number it is.

    % MON1 IS INBIT - read one byte from a device. This is how PLANC reaches a
    % monitor call: the runtime provides them as ordinary routines named MONn,
    % declared exactly like this, and the COSMOS guide's own sample does the
    % same thing one call along with IMPORT (ROUTINE VOID,VOID: MONO) for MON0.
    % The signature is copied from ND-60.117.5 appendix 0.2:
    %
    %     ROUTINE VOID, BYTE ( INTEGER ) : MON1 (dev)
    %
    % NOT MONITOR_CALL. That wants either a MON-CALL-NAMES file this machine
    % does not have, or generates 5MON_P which neither PLANC runtime defines.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, BYTE (INTEGER) : MON1 )

    % ----- THE THREE MORE THAT CHAT:CNFG NEEDS ------------------------------
    % All copied from ND-60.117.5 EN PLANC Reference Manual, the MONn appendix,
    % which is the ONLY place these signatures are written down correctly:
    %
    %     ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER ( BYTES, BYTES, INTEGER ) : MON50 (file, default, code)
    %     ROUTINE BYTE, VOID ( INTEGER )                  : MON2  (dev)
    %     ROUTINE VOID, VOID ( INTEGER )                  : MON43 (dev)
    %
    % MON50 RETURNS THE DEVICE NUMBER and raises a ROUTINEERROR on failure - it
    % has no status out-parameter, so a missing file can only be learned by
    % catching the raise. MON2 takes the byte as its IN-VALUE, which is why it
    % is called "b MON2(dev)" and not "MON2(dev, b)".
    %
    % MON43 IS CLOSE, AND IT IS NOT 54. A note in this file used to list the
    % available calls as "50/54/1/2", which reads as though 54 were the close.
    % The manual's own table says otherwise: 43B is CloseFile and 54B is
    % DeleteFile. Getting that wrong would not have failed to compile - it
    % would have deleted the user's config file every time they set a nickname.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, BYTES, INTEGER) : MON50 )
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE BYTE, VOID (INTEGER) : MON2 )
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : MON43 )

    % MON70 IS CALLCOMMAND - hand SINTRAN a line as if it had been typed.
    %
    %     ROUTINE VOID, VOID ( BYTES ) : MON70 (command)
    %
    % Needed because CREATING a file is its own monitor call, CreateFile /
    % CRALF 221B, and 221 is NOT among the MONn routines the PLANC runtime
    % provides - the appendix stops at MON263 and has no 221. MON50 opens what
    % exists; it has no size parameter and cannot make a file. See writeCnfg.
    IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES) : MON70 )

    % CreateFile, CRALF 221B - TESTED, AND IT IS NOT THERE.
    %
    %   Brl: LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED
    %   MON221...10101 U
    %
    % The comment further down says 221 "is not among the MONn routines the PLANC
    % runtime provides", and the whole CREATE-FILE-through-MON70 detour rests on
    % it. That claim had never been TESTED - the list it came from is a floor
    % rather than a fence, since MON43 and MON70 are both missing from it and
    % both link - so it was worth one build to find out. It turns out to be
    % RIGHT: there is no MON221 on this machine, and the detour is justified.
    %
    % Measured on D100 2026-08-19, by importing each and reading the linker:
    %
    %   MON43  CloseFile        resolves      (absent from the documented list)
    %   MON70  CallCommand      resolves      (absent from the documented list)
    %   MON221 CreateFile       UNDEFINED
    %   MON317 ExecuteCommand   UNDEFINED
    %
    % So creating a file from PLANC on D100 needs the command route below, or
    % MON-CALL-1BANK:BRF linked in so Monitor_Call('CreateFile',...) works, or a
    % MAC interface routine for 221 loaded ahead of the runtime.
    %
    % The IMPORT is kept as a comment, not deleted, so nobody spends the cycle
    % again:
    %   IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER4, INTEGER4) : MON221 )
    %
    % Signature straight out of the VENDOR MANUAL - ND-860228-2-EN SINTRAN III
    % Monitor Calls, page 125, its own PLANC block:
    %
    %   INTEGER4 : StartAddress, NoOfPages
    %   BYTES : FileName(0:63)
    %   ON ROUTINEERROR DO
    %       IF ErrCode > 0 THEN ...
    %   ENDON
    %   Monitor_Call('CreateFile', FileName, StartAddress, NoOfPages)
    %
    % (Monitor_Call itself does not work on D100 - see PLANC-MONITOR-CALLS.md -
    % so the same three parameters go to MON221 instead.)
    %
    % A SIXTY-FOUR BYTE NAME BUFFER, for the same reason MON70 wants eighty: a
    % PLANC BYTES argument carries its own length, so a short literal hands the
    % call a short buffer. The manual's FORTRAN form agrees - CHARACTER
    % FileName*64.
    %
    % StartAddress 0 and NoOfPages 0 both mean "an ordinary indexed file that
    % grows as it is written", which is what a one-line config file wants. PLANC
    % cannot pass an INTEGER4 as a constant, so they are variables.
    %
    % ONE DISAGREEMENT WORTH KNOWING: the repo's own
    % Developer\MON\calls\221B_CreateFile.yaml lists both sizes as INTEGER2 in
    % its parameter table, while the MANUAL's PLANC, FORTRAN (INTEGER*4) and
    % COBOL (S9(10)) forms all say four bytes, and its MAC example calls the size
    % "a double word". The manual wins here; the YAML table looks like an
    % extraction slip.
    %
    % The IMPORT is commented out because MON221 does not link on this machine -
    % see the measurement above. Restore it if MON-CALL-1BANK:BRF is ever
    % installed, or if a MAC interface routine for 221 is written.
    %   IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER4, INTEGER4) : MON221 )

    % MON317 - ExecuteCommand, UECOM 317B - IS NOT AVAILABLE ON D100. TESTED.
    %
    % It was imported here and used instead of MON70, because the Monitor Calls
    % manual recommends it ("An error message is output if an error occurs. The
    % program does not terminate") and an error message is exactly what this
    % stubborn CREATE needed. The linker answered:
    %
    %   Brl: LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED
    %   MON317...10020 U
    %
    % So the PLANC runtime on this machine carries no MON317, and the import is
    % left here as a COMMENT rather than deleted so nobody spends the cycle
    % again. Reaching UECOM would need a hand-written MAC interface routine
    % loaded ahead of PLANC-1BANK-F00.
    %
    % AND THE BUILD DID NOT FAIL. BRF-LINKER wrote a runnable CHAT:PROG with the
    % entry still undefined, @CHAT started it, and it behaved oddly in a way
    % that looked like evidence about the CREATE. It was not evidence about
    % anything. LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED is in the build for this reason and its
    % output has to be READ - nothing else fails.

    % ----- message kinds, mirroring ChatMessageKind.cs ----------------------
    INTEGER : kJoin    := 1
    INTEGER : kWelcome := 2
    INTEGER : kReject  := 3
    INTEGER : kSay     := 4
    INTEGER : kSaid    := 5
    INTEGER : kLeave   := 6
    INTEGER : kJoined  := 7
    INTEGER : kLeft    := 8
    INTEGER : kRename  := 9
    INTEGER : kRenamed := 10

    % ONE KIND, BOTH DIRECTIONS. We send it with an empty text to ask; the room
    % answers with the same kind and the members' names in the text, separated
    % by single spaces. A second kind for the reply would buy nothing - we know
    % we asked, and a room never asks us.
    %
    % This is the eleventh kind, and the note that stood here saying there was
    % no eleventh kind is now wrong and has gone. What it warned about still
    % holds and is worth keeping in plain words: kHello lived here for one day
    % as half of a handshake we invented, and it was wrong because the sender's
    % address arrives WITH the message - there was nothing to hand shake about.
    % /who is different: the membership list is real information that only the
    % room has, so there is something to ask for.
    INTEGER : kWho     := 11

    % /topic and /list. Both are one kind in both directions, same as kWho.
    % These MUST match CHATSV.PLNC, DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json and
    % ChatMessageKind.cs - all four move in the same commit.
    % 12 is Map, reserved for /map - see the note in CHATSV.PLNC.
    % /map - the machines and their trunks. ONE KIND BOTH DIRECTIONS like kWho:
    % sent with an empty text, answered with readable text. A ROOM kind, not an
    % admin one - seeing how the network is wired is not the same right as
    % rewiring it, which is what keeps it off the admin port.
    INTEGER : kMap     := 12

    INTEGER : kRooms   := 13
    INTEGER : kTopic   := 14

    % The answer to /who when this client has NO SEAT - every room and the
    % people in each. RECEIVE ONLY: the client never sends 15, it sends kWho and
    % the SERVER picks the answer, because the server is the one that knows
    % whether we have a seat. See the note beside it in CHATSV.PLNC.
    INTEGER : kAllWho  := 15

    % Replayed history, one per remembered message, sent to us alone when we
    % join. RECEIVE ONLY - the client never sends it.
    INTEGER : kHist    := 16

    % DIRECT MESSAGES - one person to one person. kDirect goes out, and the
    % other three come back: the message itself to the person it is FOR, a
    % receipt to the sender naming who it actually reached, and a refusal with
    % the reason when it reached nobody.
    INTEGER : kDirect   := 17
    INTEGER : kDirected := 18
    INTEGER : kDirSent  := 19
    INTEGER : kDirBad   := 20

    INTEGER : ownTerminal := 1        % FILE number of your own terminal - MON1 only

    % THE LDN OF THIS TERMINAL, WHICH IS NOT 1 AND NEVER WAS.
    %
    % This is the answer to the whole no-wait puzzle, and it was in the Monitor
    % Calls manual the entire time. Beside the LDN parameter of the calls that
    % take one, ND-860228-2 says in as many words:
    %
    %   "Logical device number. See appendix B. You cannot use 1 for your own
    %    terminal. Use ExecutionInfo to get its logical device number instead.
    %    File numbers are illegal."
    %
    % And the return status this program measured from TerminalNoWait was -1,
    % which the same manual defines as "error in parameters". So the call was
    % never refused, never unavailable, and never about the io flag: it was
    % handed a FILE number where an LDN belongs, and said so.
    %
    % 1 IS STILL RIGHT FOR MON1. InByte takes a file number and 1 means own
    % terminal there - that call has worked all along. Two different numbering
    % schemes, one variable doing both jobs, and the manual's own warning that
    % "file numbers are illegal" reads as a footnote until you know that is
    % exactly what you passed.
    %
    % Filled by findMyLdn at start-up. Left at 1 if that fails, which restores
    % exactly the old behaviour rather than inventing a worse one.
    INTEGER : myLdn := 1
    % BACK TO 0, AND 1 IS WORSE. BOTH VALUES MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-21.
    %
    % With 0: TerminalNoWait SUCCEEDS - no error - and MON1 still blocks, so a
    % listener sees nothing until they press return, at which point the queued
    % lines all arrive at once:
    %
    %     ANNA:
    %     ANNA: NOWAIT-PROOF-LINE
    %
    % With 1: NOTHING IS SHOWN AT ALL, not even on return. The client still
    % joins - X-C LIST-NAMES showed its seat spent - and the server still sends
    % to it: its log recorded "SV bcast sent to  2" for the very lines that
    % never appeared. So the messages arrive at a client that displays none of
    % them, which is strictly worse than the press-return behaviour.
    %
    % AND NOW IT IS UNDERSTOOD, FROM THE MANUAL RATHER THAN FROM ANOTHER GUESS.
    % ND-860228-2, NoWaitSwitch, parameter 2:
    %
    %   "Input or output flag. Use 0 for input and 1 for output."
    %
    % So 1 put the OUTPUT side into no-wait: OutByte stopped waiting for the
    % terminal to be ready and simply dropped what it could not write. "Nothing
    % is shown at all" was not a mystery, it was the documented behaviour of the
    % value we chose. 0 is not merely "the safe value" - it is the RIGHT value,
    % and there is nothing left to experiment with here.
    %
    % Two measurements, both correct, and the conclusion drawn from them was
    % still wrong, because the manual page that names the parameter was never
    % opened. Grep the Reference-Manuals BEFORE the third build.
    %
    % THE NOTE THAT USED TO SIT HERE IS NO LONGER TRUE, and it is corrected
    % rather than deleted because it would send the next reader down a dead end.
    % It said: "terminalWait, the restore on exit, calls NoWaitSwitch - the name
    % that is REFUSED on this machine - so the terminal is never actually put
    % back. That wants fixing before any more experiments with this flag."
    %
    % IT WAS FIXED. terminalWait now calls TerminalNoWait FIRST and only falls
    % back to NoWaitSwitch if that raises, which is the same order terminalNoWait
    % uses. The restore does happen. Checked 2026-08-22 by reading the routine,
    % not by assuming the note was current.
    %
    % Worth saying because this codebase has been caught by a stale comment
    % before: one claiming MONITOR_CALL by name could not work here outlived the
    % installation of the package that makes it work, and cost a day of designing
    % around a wall that was not there. A comment is a claim with a date on it.
    INTEGER : inputFlag   := 0
    INTEGER : noWaitOn    := 1        % any non-zero switches no-wait ON
    INTEGER : noWaitOff   := 0        % and zero switches it back off again
    INTEGER : parityMask  := 127      % strip the even-parity bit, see above
    INTEGER : carriageRet := 13
    INTEGER : emptyInput  := 3        % InByte in no-wait: nothing was typed

    % The idle sleep, so the poll loop is a POLL and not a SPIN. Unit type 1 is
    % basic time units of 1/50 second, so 10 is a fifth of a second: fast enough
    % that an arriving line looks instant, slow enough to cost nothing.
    %
    % CHATSV carries the hard-won version of this note: a bare loop with no
    % sleep is not an option. When XMSG died under the server, its receive
    % stopped blocking and the loop burned the machine until somebody pressed
    % ESC. The same trap is waiting here the moment the terminal stops blocking.
    INTEGER : sleepUnits := 10
    INTEGER : sleepKind  := 1
    INTEGER : sleepWhy   := 0

    % How many messages the last drain pass printed. Set by drainPort, read by
    % the main loop to decide whether the pass found anything at all.
    INTEGER : lastDrainCount := 0

    % The ErrCode from the last refused no-wait switch. Module level because a
    % PLANC local cannot be read after its ON block has run, and the number is
    % the whole point of the attempt - see terminalNoWait.
    INTEGER : firstErr := 0
    % How many messages one drain pass will take before going back to the
    % keyboard. See drainPort: the unbounded version hung on the first run.
    INTEGER : maxDrain    := 32

    INTEGER ARRAY : stack(0:1000)

    BYTES : letterBuf(0:63)           % the XSLET header XMPBLET builds
    BYTES : outBuf(0:255)             % our own message, sent after the header
    % SIZE OF inBuf, named because the read below must clamp against it. XMSG
    % reports the sender's message size; nothing stops that being larger than
    % this array, and PLANC checks no bound.
    INTEGER : inBufSize := 256
    BYTES : inBuf(0:255)              % what arrives, inBufSize bytes
    % THE LINE BEING TYPED, and its size is a named constant because the loop
    % below has to test against it. PLANC does not check array bounds: without
    % the test, an 81st character is written past the end of this array and into
    % whatever the compiler laid out next - which here is roomName, typedLen,
    % serverMagic and joined. A user holding a key down would quietly corrupt
    % the program's own state.
    % THE LONGEST ROOM OR NICK NAME a command may carry. This was USED by
    % handleCommand and never DECLARED - the compiler answered ILLEGAL SYNTAX
    % "MAXNAMELEN" twice, the loader pass built CHAT:PROG regardless, and every
    % /join and /nick then answered "too long" for any argument at all. A build
    % with errors still produces a program; only the LISTING says otherwise.
    INTEGER : maxNameLen := 16

    % Must match maxTopicLen in CHATSV.PLNC. The server truncates at this
    % length; the client refuses at it, so our own user is told rather than
    % quietly clipped.
    INTEGER : maxTopicLen := 64
    INTEGER : maxTyped := 80
    BYTES : typed(0:79)               % 0:maxTyped-1

    % One character wide, so a BYTE can be printed as a CHARACTER: 'ALn' formats
    % a STRING, and handing it a byte prints the byte's NUMBER instead. See
    % showArrived, where that turned RONNY into 8279787889 on screen.
    BYTES : oneChar(0:0)

    % A STRING LITERAL CANNOT BE STORED INTO A BYTES ELEMENT and the compiler
    % blames the ARRAY when you try, so every literal character written into a
    % buffer lives in a one-element BYTES and is copied byte to byte.
    BYTES : spaceByte := ' '
    BYTES : slashByte := '/'

    % TWENTY-ONE BYTES OF STORAGE, NOT A LITERAL - and this was a live defect,
    % not tidying. It was BYTES : roomName := 'CHAT-LOBBY', exactly ten bytes,
    % while /join accepts an argument up to maxNameLen = 16 and copies it in
    % here. Eleven characters onwards wrote past the end, into whatever the
    % loader laid out next. The comment on "typed" above already warns that
    % roomName is what an overlong typed line runs into; roomName had the same
    % fault pointing at whatever follows IT.
    %
    % Twenty-one is the prefix (5) plus the room's own limit (16).
    BYTES : roomName(0:20)
    INTEGER : lenRoomName := 10

    % THE PREFIX EVERY ROOM'S REGISTERED NAME CARRIES, and forgetting it is
    % why /join never worked.
    %
    % ChatRooms.NamePrefix in the C# library says "CHAT-", and ChatRooms.NameFor
    % builds "CHAT-" + the room's short name in upper case. So the LOBBY room is
    % registered with XROUT as CHAT-LOBBY. /join used to copy the typed argument
    % in raw, so "/join GENERAL" asked XROUT for a name called GENERAL - which no
    % room ever registers. XROUT answered "no such name" and the client sat in
    % the room it was already in, which is exactly the reported symptom.
    %
    % The startup default below is built the same way, from the prefix and the
    % short name, so there is one rule and not two.
    % THE SERVICE NAME. A CONSTANT - it is not built from the room any more.
    %
    % This used to be roomPrefix := 'CHAT-', and setRoom pasted the typed room
    % onto it to make a PORT NAME, so joining a room meant addressing a
    % different port. One port now; the room rides in the message. See
    % the_room_model in DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json.
    BYTES : servName := '*CHAT'
    INTEGER : lenServName := 5

    % The room joined at start-up when nobody says otherwise. SHORT name: the
    % prefix is added by setRoom, the same as for a typed /join.
    BYTES : homeRoom := 'LOBBY'
    INTEGER : lenHomeRoom := 5
    BYTES : hereSystem := ''          % length 0 means the LOCAL system
    INTEGER : lenHereSystem := 0

    % SIXTEEN BYTES OF STORAGE, NOT A LITERAL, and the change is not cosmetic.
    %
    % This was BYTES : myName := 'RONNY', which reserves five bytes and exactly
    % five. Asking for a longer name would have written past the end of it into
    % whatever the loader happened to put next, with nothing to stop it - PLANC
    % checks no array bound. The same defect, in the other direction, is what
    % the guard in CHATSV.handleMessage exists for.
    %
    % Sixteen is the ROOM's number: ChatRoom.MaxNicknameLength says sixteen and
    % CHATSV drops anything longer, so a name this side cannot send is one the
    % other side would refuse anyway.
    BYTES : myName(0:15)
    INTEGER : lenMyName := 5

    % The name used when nobody types one AND nothing was remembered. Kept as a
    % separate literal because myName is now a buffer and has to be filled at
    % start-up rather than declared full - see setupName.
    % THE LAST RESORT, and it should not be a person's name. A hardcoded RONNY
    % turning up on somebody else's terminal is the same kind of wrong as the
    % OLAV that arrived from a config file written during testing. It is only
    % reached when SINTRAN itself will not say who is logged in.
    BYTES : defaultName := 'USER'
    INTEGER : lenDefault := 4

    % Where GetUserName writes. SINTRAN pads the answer, so the name ends at the
    % first space and the buffer is deliberately wider than maxNameLen.
    BYTES : sintranUser(0:31)

    % How much of sintranUser is the name. ZERO means SINTRAN would not say, and
    % that is the state every check below must cope with - see readCnfg, which
    % refuses to trust a remembered nickname when it cannot tell whose it is.
    INTEGER : lenSintranUser

    % THE OTHER FOUR PARAMETERS OF GetUserName, and the reason they exist here.
    %
    % The call was first written with ONE argument and answered "refused" every
    % time. The OVERVIEW table lists only the first parameter -
    %
    %     | **GetUserName** (GUSNA 214B) | | | | | |
    %     | 1. User name                 | STR | O | | . | . |
    %
    % - and the DETAIL PAGE gives the real shape:
    %
    %     Monitor_Call('GetUserName', UserName(1:16), DirIndex, UserIndex,
    %                  RemoteFlag, RemoteSystem(1:64))
    %
    % FIVE arguments. The same mistake as StartRTProgram: reading the summary
    % row instead of opening the entry. A monitor call given the wrong number of
    % arguments is REFUSED, exactly as if it were not permitted, so the two look
    % identical from the outside and one gets mistaken for the other.
    INTEGER : dirIndex
    INTEGER : userIndex
    INTEGER : remoteFlag
    BYTES : remoteSystem(0:63)

    % ----- CHAT:CNFG, the remembered nickname -------------------------------
    % IN THE USER'S OWN FILE AREA, which is the whole point: OLAV and ANNA log
    % in as themselves and each gets their own. Nothing here names a user -
    % SINTRAN resolves an unqualified name against whoever is logged in, so the
    % separation is free and cannot be got wrong.
    %
    % MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100: user OLAV, "@CREATE-FILE CHAT:CNFG,1"
    % succeeded and "@CHAT" ran from that user's terminal, so both halves - a
    % writable file of one's own, and permission to run the client - are real
    % rather than assumed.
    %
    % THE FILE IS ONE LINE: the nickname, ended by a carriage return.
    % Deliberately not a key=value format. There is one setting, a reader that
    % has to parse nothing cannot misparse anything, and a person can fix it
    % with any editor on the machine.
    BYTES : cnfgName   := 'CHAT'
    BYTES : cnfgType   := 'CNFG'

    % MAKING THE FILE IS A COMMAND, NOT AN OPEN, and that cost a build cycle.
    %
    % The first version passed the name QUOTED to MON50 - '"CHAT"' - on the
    % rule that quoting is what asks SINTRAN to create. MEASURED 2026-08-18 as
    % user ANNA, who had no config file: the open did not fail loudly, but
    % every MON2 after it raised, and afterwards LIST-FILES showed ANNA still
    % owned no files at all. Nothing was created.
    %
    % The reason is in the manual rather than in the quoting: creating a file
    % is its OWN monitor call, CreateFile / CRALF 221B, and 221 is not among
    % the MONn routines the PLANC runtime provides. MON50 opens; it has no size
    % parameter and cannot make a file that is not there.
    %
    % So the file is made the way a person would make it, by handing SINTRAN
    % the command - MON70, CallCommand. One page, which is far more than one
    % line needs.
    %
    % NO QUOTES, AND THE QUOTES ARE WHY THIS NEVER WORKED.
    %
    % This read 'CREATE-FILE "CHAT:CNFG",1' from the day it was written, and
    % measured on D100 2026-08-19 that command answers
    %
    %   @CREATE-FILE "CHAT:CNFG",1
    %   ILLEGAL CHARACTER IN PARAMETER
    %
    % while the unquoted form succeeds silently. So the file was NEVER created,
    % every save failed, and CHAT:CNFG did not exist for any user.
    %
    % THE TRAP IS THAT QUOTES MEAN OPPOSITE THINGS ON THE TWO ROUTES:
    %
    %   MON50(file, ...)   quoting the name means CREATE IT IF ABSENT
    %   CREATE-FILE <name> quoting the name is an ILLEGAL CHARACTER
    %
    % The quotes here were carried over from the MON50 attempt described above,
    % where they were correct, into the command that replaced it, where they are
    % fatal. A local filespec takes no quotes; the quoting rule is for REMOTE
    % ones, where the name goes to another machine's command processor.
    %
    % AND THE COMMAND MUST END WITH AN APOSTROPHE, which is the second half of
    % why this never worked. Removing the quotes alone was not enough - measured
    % on D100 2026-08-19, CHAT:CNFG still did not appear.
    %
    % The Monitor Calls manual's own worked example gives the rule, in its
    % ASSEMBLY-500 section under CallCommand:
    %
    %   Command : STRINGDATA 'CLOSE-FILE 102'''
    %
    % - that trailing triple apostrophe is the string's closing quote plus ONE
    % apostrophe character, so the command handed to SINTRAN is
    % "CLOSE-FILE 102'". The apostrophe TERMINATES the command; without it the
    % command processor keeps reading past the end of the buffer into whatever
    % follows, and the command never runs as written.
    %
    % In PLANC an apostrophe inside a literal is written doubled, so the line
    % below is CREATE-FILE CHAT:CNFG,1 followed by one apostrophe.
    BYTES : cnfgMake := 'CREATE-FILE CHAT:CNFG,1'''

    % EIGHTY BYTES, BECAUSE THAT IS THE BUFFER MON70 IS DEFINED TO TAKE - and
    % this, not the quoting and not the terminator, is why the file was never
    % created. Both earlier fixes were real and neither changed the outcome.
    %
    % The Monitor Calls manual gives the PLANC form of CallCommand as
    %
    %   BYTES : Command(0:79)
    %
    % an EIGHTY-BYTE ARRAY. A PLANC BYTES argument carries its own length, so
    % handing MON70 a 25-byte literal hands it a 25-byte buffer where it expects
    % eighty - the same defect that made XROUT look up a room name with eleven
    % bytes of leftovers on the end, in the other direction.
    %
    % So the command is COPIED into a full-size buffer, apostrophe-terminated,
    % and the rest left as spaces. See makeCnfg.
    INTEGER : lenCnfgMake := 24
    BYTES : cmdBuf(0:79)
    INTEGER : cmdBufSize := 80
    BYTES : spaceChar := ' '

    % ----- CreateFile, the direct route -------------------------------------
    % The FULL filespec, name and type together - the manual's example creates
    % 'NAME-STORAGE:DATA' in one string - in the 64-byte buffer MON221 expects.
    % One line of CHAT:CNFG, read whole before any of it is believed. Wider than
    % maxNameLen because it holds OWNER:NICK, not just a nickname.
    BYTES : cnfgLine(0:63)

    BYTES : cnfgFull := 'CHAT:CNFG'
    INTEGER : lenCnfgFull := 9
    BYTES : nameBuf(0:63)
    INTEGER : nameBufSize := 64
    INTEGER4 : startAddr := 0         % 0 = let SINTRAN place it
    INTEGER4 : noOfPages := 0         % 0 = indexed, grows when written

    % Access codes for MON50, from the Monitor Calls manual's own table:
    %   0  sequential write
    %   1  sequential read
    INTEGER : openRead  := 1
    INTEGER : openWrite := 0

    % A "nameRemembered" BOOLEAN stood here, to word the prompt differently for
    % a remembered name than for the built-in one. It was set and never read.
    % The prompt shows the actual name either way, which answers the same
    % question better than a different wording would have.

    INTEGER : myPort
    INTEGER : returnStatus
    INTEGER : offSet, uLength, wLength
    INTEGER : msgType, remotePortHash, nBytes, readLength, readAmount
    INTEGER : typedLen := 0
    INTEGER : ch
    % MON1's out-value is a BYTE, so it lands here first and is widened into ch
    % before the parity bit is masked off.
    BYTE : rawCh
    INTEGER4 : senderMagic
    % ZERO until the welcome arrives and reveals the real address. It is set
    % explicitly rather than left to whatever the loader put there, because the
    % joined test below is what keeps it from ever being used - and a guard is
    % worth more when the thing it guards is a known value.
    INTEGER4 : serverMagic := 0
    % An INTEGER4 myMagic lived here to hold our own port's magic number, read
    % with XMPFP2M purely so a diagnostic could print it. Both are gone - see
    % the note in chatClient for what they settled (the reply address was
    % correct all along) and why that ended the need for them.
    BOOLEAN : joined := FALSE

    % WHICH MACHINE THIS CLIENT IS ON, as the server said in the welcome.
    %
    % A CLIENT CANNOT WORK THIS OUT FOR ITSELF. It has no system number of its
    % own and nothing in the join tells it - the server knows, because it asked
    % the kernel, and now says so in the welcome's text where there was never
    % anything before.
    %
    % Empty until the welcome arrives, and empty is a fine answer: the screen
    % shows the nickname alone rather than a machine name that would be a
    % guess. It is 'D100' on a machine with no name and 'FJELL' on one that
    % has been given one, and this client does not care which - it prints what
    % it was told.
    BYTES   : myMachine(0:15)
    INTEGER : lenMyMach := 0

    % Has the user CHOSEN a name with /nick this session?
    %
    % This exists to tell two refusals apart, because they arrive on the SAME
    % kReject and mean completely different things to the person reading them:
    %
    %   FALSE - we joined under the name SINTRAN gave us, and the room says it is
    %           taken. That means the SAME SINTRAN USER is already in the room
    %           from ANOTHER TERMINAL. Say exactly that.
    %   TRUE  - the user typed /nick ANNA and ANNA is taken. Telling them they are
    %           "already in from another terminal" would be FALSE and confusing -
    %           somebody else has that name.
    %
    % Set once, never cleared: after any /nick, every later refusal is about a
    % name the user picked.
    BOOLEAN : userChoseNick := FALSE

    % Said once. A refused sleep every fifth of a second is a flood that hides
    % itself, so idleSleep speaks the first time and stays quiet after.
    BOOLEAN : sleepBroken := FALSE
    % TRUE once RETURN has been seen, so a line half typed when the port
    % needs draining survives the trip round the outer loop.
    BOOLEAN : lineReady := FALSE
    BOOLEAN : running := TRUE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build one of our messages into outBuf. Returns its total length.
    %
    % Same layout as ChatMessage.cs, which is the only specification it has:
    % kind, name length, name, two length bytes, text.
    %
    % THE EXACT BYTES ARE PINNED in
    % SRC/Xmsg.Chat.Tests/ChatMessageGoldenBytesTests.cs - a Said, a Renamed, a
    % Join and a 300-character line that proves the text length is big-endian.
    % There is no shared code between that and this, only those goldens, so if
    % the layout ever changes both sides change in the SAME commit or they
    % disagree in silence.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the JOIN. Same layout as any other message - kind, nickname, text -
    % but the TEXT carries the ROOM this time.
    %
    % THAT IS WHY THIS COSTS NO WIRE CHANGE. A Join always had an empty text
    % field; only what goes in it is new. message_prefix does not move and the
    % golden bytes keep their shape - just their content for Join.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildJoin
        INTEGER : i, at
        kJoin =: outBuf(0)
        lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
            myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        % Text length, high byte then low. A room name is far short of 256.
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        lenRoomName =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
            roomName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : buildOwn(kind, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kind =: outBuf(0)
        lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        % SUBSCRIPT FROM ZERO. myName is a BYTES declared from a literal, so its
        % first character is at index 0. This said myName(i) and that is why the
        % server saw the nickname as ONNY, not RONNY: the R was skipped and one
        % byte was read past the end of the string. MEASURED 2026-08-18 in a raw
        % dump of the arriving letter - the length byte said 5 and the five bytes
        % were 'O','N','N','Y',0. Nothing reports this; PLANC checks no bound,
        % and a wrong name is not an error to anyone. Compare cmdIs below, which
        % has always had it right with word(i - 1).
        FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
            myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        textLength =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
            typed(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        % The out-value goes BEFORE the keyword. ND-60.117.5 prints it as
        % "result RETURN" in the complex-addition example; writing "RETURN at"
        % makes the compiler read RETURN as a type and answer
        % ILLEGAL DATA TYPE "RETURN".
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Same message as buildOwn, but the text is taken from PART of the typed
    % line rather than all of it.
    %
    % buildOwn always copies typed(0) onwards, which is right for a Say - the
    % whole line IS the text. A command argument is not: "/topic the coffee is
    % on" has to send only what follows the verb, and copying from zero would
    % put the command itself on the wire.
    %
    % from is a ZERO-BASED index into typed, matching buildOwn's typed(i - 1).
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildOwnFrom(kind, from, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kind =: outBuf(0)
        lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
            myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        textLength =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
            typed(from + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build a DIRECT MESSAGE out of "NAME rest of the line".
    %
    % THE WIRE WANTS "TARGET/message" AND THE USER TYPES "TARGET message", so
    % the first space becomes a slash. That is the whole conversion, and doing
    % it here rather than asking the user to type the slash is deliberate: the
    % slash is a protocol detail, and a person should not have to know one to
    % send somebody a line.
    %
    % A TARGET CONTAINING A SLASH IS REFUSED by the caller, because it would
    % split in the wrong place at the far end and deliver to a name nobody has.
    %
    % from is a ZERO-BASED index into typed, matching buildOwn.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildTell(from, tgtLen, msgLen)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kDirect =: outBuf(0)
        lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
            myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        tgtLen + 1 + msgLen =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at

        FOR i IN 1:tgtLen DO
            typed(from + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
        at + 1 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:msgLen DO
            typed(from + tgtLen + i) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build a RENAME, which does NOT have the same shape as every other
    % message and is the one place buildOwn cannot be used.
    %
    % THE NEW NAME GOES IN THE NAME FIELD, and the text is empty. This client
    % had it the other way round - new name in the TEXT, current name in the
    % NAME field - and the rename was thrown away in silence every time.
    %
    % MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100: "/nick OLAV" was accepted by the client,
    % sent, and changed nothing; the room went on saying RONNY. Nothing was
    % printed by either side, because the path it took is a deliberate
    % do-nothing:
    %
    %   the server reads the new name from the NAME field;
    %   the client had put the CURRENT name there;
    %   so sameName(slot, ...) matched, and the server took its
    %   "already your name: not news" RETURN, which prints nothing.
    %
    % TWO WRONG THINGS AGREEING TO DO NOTHING. Each side was self-consistent
    % and the pair was broken, which is why it survived a working build.
    %
    % THE C# PAIR IS THE ARBITER and it is unambiguous - ChatClient.Rename
    % sends "new ChatMessage(ChatMessageKind.Rename, nickname, string.Empty)",
    % the NEW nickname in the name field and an EMPTY text, and
    % ChatServer.HandleRename reads it back with _room.TryRename(id,
    % message.Nickname, ...). The PLANC SERVER already matched that. Only this
    % side did not.
    %
    % The sender is identified by its MAGIC NUMBER, not by the name it sends -
    % findByMagic(senderMagic) is what picks the seat - so the name field is
    % free to carry the new name and nothing is lost by not sending the old one.
    %
    % myName is deliberately NOT changed here. Our own name changes when the
    % room says so with kRenamed, so a refused rename leaves us as we were.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : buildRename(nameFrom, newLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kRename =: outBuf(0)
        newLength =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:newLength DO
            typed(nameFrom + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        % An empty text, spelled out as its two length bytes.
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Say what "no access" actually means, because the number alone sends
    % people to the wire.
    %
    % XRUNN and XRNRO are the same fault seen from two sides, and BOTH mean the
    % peer has not been told to trust this system. XRUNN reads "unknown name of
    % server or system" and it is the SYSTEM half of that phrase - not the
    % server half - which is what sent two earlier investigations chasing
    % *CHATSV when the room was fine.
    %
    % Everything else looks healthy while this is wrong: the peer lists the
    % system, LIST-SYSTEMS says ON NET, LIST-LINKS says State Run with
    % thousands of frames received. Nothing comes back because the rejection
    % happens before XROUT is asked, so there is no error to send.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showFriendAdvice
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL48', 'the PEER has not defined us as a FRIEND system.$')
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL26', 'on that machine:  @X-COMM$')
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL47', '  X-C: DEFINE-FRIEND-SYSTEM   System? <our no>$')
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL44', 'check it with LIST-ROUTING-INFO - it prints$')
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL40', '  "T: *, but no access to system NNNNN"$')
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL45', 'NOTE: DEF-REMOTE CLEARS the friend entry, so$')
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL46', 'the name comes first and the friend AFTER it.$')
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER) : showStatus(where, code)
        % NOT "offset" - the module already has a global offSet for the letter, and
        % PLANC ignores case, so that spelling collided with it and the compiler
        % said IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED.
        %
        % AND IT IS A RANGE, NOT A PLAIN INTEGER, because this is what CASE
        % selects on. ND-60.117.5 section 6.3: the CASE expression "may have a
        % maximum of 256 different values, and must be either an enumeration
        % with a maximum of 256 different values, or integers ranging between 0
        % and 255", with the note that an INTEGER RANGE used this way must have
        % a lower bound of 0. A plain INTEGER spans -32768..32767, so the
        % compiler answered OUT OF RANGE on both CASE statements - a WARNING,
        % which is exactly the kind that gets skimmed past.
        INTEGER RANGE (0:255) : xmxOffset

        OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', where)
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', ' status ')
        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', code)
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', ' ')

        IF code = XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL12', 'XMOK - done$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF code < 0 THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', 'raw kernel error, not a library one$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF code < XMXKXXX THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'message type, not an error$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % XROUT ERRORS SIT ABOVE THE KERNEL ONES, at their own base
        % XRXXX = 16960 = 41100B, so this test has to come FIRST - the XMX
        % block below would otherwise decode 16972 as XMX offset 76 and say
        % nothing useful. Two of these are the same fault wearing two names,
        % and it is the one that costs a whole evening every time, so it gets
        % told in full rather than named.
        IF code >= XRXXX THEN
            code - XRXXX =: xmxOffset
            CASE xmxOffset
                INCASE 2
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL39', 'XRUNN unknown name of server OR SYSTEM$')
                    showFriendAdvice
                INCASE 12
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XRNRO no access to remote system$')
                    showFriendAdvice
                INCASE 0
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', 'XRSOK ok - not an error$')
                INCASE 1
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'XRISN illegal service number$')
                INCASE 3
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL41', 'XRDDF another port already has this name$')
                INCASE 4
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'XRNSP no space left for names$')
                INCASE 7
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XRUNM unknown magic number$')
                INCASE 10
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', 'XRPRV caller was not privileged$')
                INCASE 11
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', 'XRISY illegal system number parameter$')
                ELSE
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL23', 'XROUT error, XR offset ')
                    OUTPUT(1, 'I6', xmxOffset)
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            ENDCASE
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        code - XMXKXXX =: xmxOffset
        CASE xmxOffset
            INCASE 0
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL39', 'XMXENTM not terminated - nothing there$')
            INCASE 1
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL31', 'XMXENOT no more XT-blocks free$')
            INCASE 2
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', 'XMXEIRM non-local remote port illegal$')
            INCASE 4
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', 'XMXETMM task allowed no more memory$')
            INCASE 5
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XMXENIM facility not implemented$')
            INCASE 6
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL39', 'XMXEIBP illegal message buffer pointer$')
            INCASE 7
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XMXEBNY message buffer not yours$')
            INCASE 8
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL41', 'XMXEIRT illegal function for RT programs$')
            INCASE 9
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', 'XMXENOP no more ports available$')
            INCASE 10
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL42', 'XMXEIDR function not available to drivers$')
            INCASE 11
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XMXENDM no default message$')
            INCASE 12
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL35', 'XMXEMCH message is already chained$')
            INCASE 13
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'XMXEBFC message is in a queue$')
            INCASE 14
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL35', 'XMXEAIN kernel already initialised$')
            INCASE 15
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL35', 'XMXEBNC return of a bounce message$')
            INCASE 16
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL26', 'XMXEWNA write not allowed$')
            INCASE 17
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XMXENVI no valid indirect buffer$')
            INCASE 18
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'XMXEILF illegal function code$')
            INCASE 19
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'XMXEIMA invalid magic number$')
            INCASE 20
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XMXEMFL message space full$')
            INCASE 21
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'XMXEILM illegal message size$')
            INCASE 22
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL28', 'XMXEIPN illegal port number$')
            INCASE 23
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL42', 'XMXEPRV privileged function, no privilege$')
            INCASE 24
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL34', 'XMXEPVR privilege request refused$')
            INCASE 25
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', 'XMXERNA remote system not available$')
            ELSE
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL26', 'library error, XMX offset ')
                OUTPUT(1, 'I6', xmxOffset)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
        ENDCASE
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Read the remembered nickname out of CHAT:CNFG into myName.
    %
    % A MISSING FILE IS NOT AN ERROR and must not look like one. The first time
    % anybody runs this there is no file, and that is the ordinary case rather
    % than a fault - so a failed open leaves myName alone and says nothing. The
    % caller has already put RONNY there.
    %
    % ROUTINEERROR IS THE ONLY WAY TO LEARN THE OPEN FAILED. MON50 does not
    % return a status; it returns a device number and raises on failure, the
    % same as MON1 does at end of file. Without the handler a first run - which
    % is every user's first run - would unwind the program before it joined.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : readCnfg
        INTEGER : dev, n
        INTEGER : c
        INTEGER : i, k, m
        BOOLEAN : openFailed
        BOOLEAN : haveColon

        % A FLAG, NOT A MAGIC VALUE OF dev - see the long note in writeCnfg.
        % A failed open leaves 46 in dev, not 0, so "IF dev = 0" never fired and
        % this routine went on to READ from a device it had never opened.
        0 =: dev
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            % No file, no name, nothing to say.
            TRUE =: openFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: openFailed
        MON50(cnfgName, cnfgType, openRead) =: dev

        IF openFailed THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % READ THE WHOLE LINE FIRST, then decide. It is now OWNER:NICK rather
        % than a bare nickname, and the owner has to be checked before a single
        % byte of it is allowed near myName.
        0 =: n
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            % End of file arrives this way, and on a one-line file it is the
            % NORMAL ending rather than a fault: a name with no carriage
            % return after it is still a name. Zero stops the loop below.
            0 =: c
        ENDON
        DO
            0 =: c
            MON1(dev) =: c
            c AND parityMask =: c

            % Stop at the end of the line. Everything after it is somebody
            % else's business - a second line, or a comment they added.
            IF c < 32 THEN
                0 =: c
            ENDIF

            IF c >< 0 THEN
                % The guard IS the bound - PLANC checks no array subscript, so
                % this is the only thing between a long line and whatever the
                % compiler laid out after cnfgLine.
                IF n < 63 THEN
                    c =: cnfgLine(n)
                    n + 1 =: n
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
            WHILE c >< 0
        ENDDO

        MON43(dev)

        % A FILE THAT EXISTS BUT IS EMPTY KEEPS THE DEFAULT. It is the state a
        % bare CREATE-FILE leaves behind, so it is a state real users will have.
        IF n = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- WHOSE NICKNAME IS THIS? ---------------------------------------
        %
        % THE BUG THIS EXISTS TO KILL. CHAT:CNFG lives under whoever ran the
        % program, and on this machine that is SYSTEM for everybody. So ONE
        % person's /nick became EVERYONE'S name: logging in as SYSTEM and
        % starting the client came up as OLAV - a name typed during testing days
        % earlier, on a different account. Nothing on screen explained it, and
        % the built-in default and the SINTRAN user name were both innocent.
        %
        % The file now records WHO chose the nickname:  OWNER:NICK
        %
        % A file with no colon is the OLD format. It is exactly the stale
        % stranger's nickname this is meant to stop, so it is IGNORED rather
        % than migrated - the next writeCnfg replaces it with an owned one, and
        % the machine heals itself without anyone deleting a file by hand.
        0 =: k
        FALSE =: haveColon
        DO
            WHILE k < n AND NOT haveColon
            IF cnfgLine(k) = 58 THEN
                TRUE =: haveColon
            ELSE
                k + 1 =: k
            ENDIF
        ENDDO

        IF NOT haveColon THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % No SINTRAN name means no way to prove ownership, so nothing is
        % trusted. Better to join as USER than as a stranger.
        IF lenSintranUser = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF k >< lenSintranUser THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % DO/WHILE, NOT "FOR i IN 0:(k - 1)". A computed range that comes out
        % BACKWARDS when k is 0 is not something to guess about in a language
        % that checks no array bound - and this file already uses this shape
        % everywhere else, so it is also the local idiom.
        0 =: i
        DO
            WHILE i < k
            IF cnfgLine(i) >< sintranUser(i) THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            i + 1 =: i
        ENDDO

        % ---- it is ours, so take it ----------------------------------------
        % k is the colon, so the nickname runs from k+1 up to n-1.
        0 =: m
        k + 1 =: i
        DO
            WHILE i < n
            IF m < maxNameLen THEN
                cnfgLine(i) =: myName(m)
                m + 1 =: m
            ENDIF
            i + 1 =: i
        ENDDO

        IF m > 0 THEN
            m =: lenMyName
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Write myName back to CHAT:CNFG, so the next run offers it.
    %
    % PLAIN OPEN FIRST, CREATE ONLY IF THAT FAILS, and the order is the point.
    % A quoted name asks SINTRAN to CREATE, and a create at a name that already
    % exists is refused - measured on the FA wire as SINTRAN error 62, "File
    % already exists". Trying the create first would therefore fail on every
    % run after the first. Trying the plain open first fails only on the very
    % first, which is exactly when a create is what we want.
    %
    % NOTHING HERE IS FATAL. A user with no space, or a read-only area, still
    % gets a working chat - they just get asked their name every time. Saying
    % so once is better than either failing or pretending it worked.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Ask SINTRAN to create CHAT:CNFG, through a FULL-SIZE command buffer.
    %
    % MON70 takes BYTES(0:79) - see the note on cmdBuf. The command text is
    % copied in, the rest filled with spaces, and the apostrophe already on the
    % end of cnfgMake terminates it so the padding is never read as parameters.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : makeCnfg
        INTEGER : i

        % CREATE THE FILE WITH THE CALL THAT CREATES FILES.
        %
        % The command-processor detour is gone. It existed because CreateFile
        % 221B has no MONn routine - measured, MON221...10101 U - and the only
        % other route needed a library that was not on the machine.
        %
        % IT IS ON THE MACHINE NOW: MON-CALL-1B-A00:BRF, the SINTRAN III Monitor
        % Call Package (ND-210913), installed 2026-08-19 and added to the LOAD
        % line in CHATCC:MODE between this program and PLANC-1BANK-F00. That
        % library supplies 5MON_P, which is what MONITOR_CALL compiles into, so
        % every one of the 230 monitor calls is now reachable - not just the 54
        % with a MONn routine.
        %
        % Parameters per ND-860228-2-EN page 125: the FULL filespec in a 64-byte
        % buffer, then StartAddress and NoOfPages, both INTEGER4. Zero and zero
        % mean an ordinary indexed file that grows as it is written.
        FOR i IN 1:nameBufSize DO
            spaceChar(0) =: nameBuf(i - 1)
        ENDFOR

        FOR i IN 1:lenCnfgFull DO
            cnfgFull(i - 1) =: nameBuf(i - 1)
        ENDFOR

        MONITOR_CALL(221B, nameBuf, startAddr, noOfPages)
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : writeCnfg
        INTEGER : dev, i
        BYTE : b
        BOOLEAN : saveFailed
        BOOLEAN : openFailed

        % A FLAG, NOT A MAGIC VALUE OF dev - AND THIS WAS THE BUG.
        %
        % This used to be "0 =: dev" in the handler and "IF dev = 0 THEN create"
        % below. MEASURED 2026-08-19, printing dev straight after a failed open
        % on a file that does not exist:
        %
        %   CNFG: open1 dev=    46
        %
        % FORTY-SIX, not zero. 46 is SINTRAN's "no such file" - the Monitor Calls
        % manual's own PASCAL example tests exactly that: "IF ErrCode = 46 THEN
        % ... CreateFile(...)". So the test for "the open failed" never fired,
        % the create was NEVER REACHED, and the program went straight on to
        % write bytes to a device number it had never been given.
        %
        % That is the whole of the CHAT:CNFG defect. The quoting, the apostrophe
        % terminator and the command buffer size were all real faults in a create
        % that was never called in the first place.
        %
        % A BOOLEAN cannot be confused with a device number, which is the point.
        %
        % AND THE FLAG IS CLEARED **AFTER** THE ON BLOCK, NOT BEFORE IT.
        % Measured 2026-08-19 with the flag cleared before: both opens reported
        % failure while returning a perfectly good device number -
        %
        %   CNFG: open1 dev=    69
        %   CNFG: open2 dev=    69
        %   CHAT: cannot save the nickname
        %
        % - so the handler body had run even though nothing failed. Clearing
        % after the block is correct whichever way the ON statement behaves: if
        % the body only runs on an error it is unchanged, and if it also runs
        % once where it stands, the clear undoes it before the call.
        0 =: dev
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: openFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: openFailed

        MON50(cnfgName, cnfgType, openWrite) =: dev

        IF openFailed THEN
            % Not there yet. Make it, then open it for real.
            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                % A create that fails is not worth its own message - the open
                % below will fail too, and that one already has one.
            ENDON
            makeCnfg

            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                TRUE =: openFailed
            ENDON
            FALSE =: openFailed
            MON50(cnfgName, cnfgType, openWrite) =: dev
        ENDIF

        IF openFailed THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL25', 'cannot save the nickname$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ONE MESSAGE, NOT ONE PER BYTE. The first version put this handler
        % around the write loop and printed inside it, so a failed save said
        % "nickname not fully saved" five times - once per character of ANNA
        % plus the carriage return. A handler that fires per byte must not
        % print per byte; it sets a flag and the caller speaks once.
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: saveFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: saveFailed

        % OWNER FIRST, THEN A COLON, THEN THE NICKNAME.
        %
        % The file is written as  OWNER:NICK  so the next run can tell whether
        % the nickname is the reader's to use. CHAT:CNFG lives under whoever ran
        % the program - SYSTEM for everybody on this machine - so without the
        % owner one person's /nick silently became everyone's name. See the long
        % note in readCnfg.
        %
        % If SINTRAN would not say who we are there is no owner to record, so
        % the colon is written with nothing before it. readCnfg then finds an
        % owner length of zero, which cannot match any real user, and ignores the
        % nickname. That is the right way round: an unowned nickname is exactly
        % the thing we refuse to inherit.
        FOR i IN 1:lenSintranUser DO
            sintranUser(i - 1) =: b
            b MON2(dev)
        ENDFOR

        58 =: b
        b MON2(dev)

        FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
            myName(i - 1) =: b
            b MON2(dev)
        ENDFOR

        % THE CARRIAGE RETURN MATTERS. readCnfg stops at the first byte below
        % 32, and a SINTRAN text file is expected to have one - without it the
        % last line runs into whatever follows.
        carriageRet =: b
        b MON2(dev)

        % CLOSE, ALWAYS - including after a failed write. A file opened for
        % sequential write is truncated at CLOSE, not at OPEN, so a program
        % that opens and never closes leaves the OLD contents in place and the
        % save silently does nothing.
        MON43(dev)

        IF saveFailed THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL25', 'nickname not fully saved$')
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Settle who we are, before the join goes out. NOTHING IS ASKED.
    %
    % THE PROMPT THAT USED TO BE HERE IS GONE, and the reason is worth keeping
    % because it was a real design mistake rather than a bug. It asked
    % "nickname?" on every single run, which is the wrong shape for a chat
    % client on a machine where you have already told SINTRAN who you are by
    % logging in. Being asked your name again, every time, by a program running
    % in your own file area, is nagging.
    %
    % The order of preference is now:
    %
    %   1. CHAT:CNFG in your own file area - what /nick last settled on;
    %   2. the built-in fallback, for a user who has never renamed.
    %
    % THE FALLBACK SHOULD BE THE SINTRAN USER NAME and is not yet. That needs
    % GetUserName (GUSNA 214B), and 214 is NOT among the MONn routines the
    % PLANC runtime provides - the appendix stops at MON263 and has no 214, the
    % same gap that CreateFile 221B falls into. The manual gives the way round
    % it: "Any monitor calls not listed here, may be called from a PLANC
    % routine if a suitable interface routine is constructed by the user. If
    % this is done, the user must load the interface routine BEFORE the PLANC
    % runtime library." MAC is installed on this machine, so that door is open
    % - it is the next piece of work, not a closed one.
    %
    % Changing the name is /nick, and the room has to agree to it: the save
    % happens when the SERVER confirms with kRenamed, never when the request is
    % sent. See showArrived.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % ASK SINTRAN WHO IS LOGGED IN.
    %
    % GetUserName (GUSNA 214B), one STR out-parameter - Reference-Manuals/
    % ND-860228-2-EN SINTRAN III Monitor Calls.md. Ronny asked the obvious
    % question: "where does the name OLAV come from when I start chat as the
    % system user?" It came off disk, from a nickname saved during testing, and
    % failing that from a name compiled into the program. The client had never
    % once asked the machine.
    %
    % THE COMMENT ABOVE setupName USED TO SAY THIS COULD NOT BE DONE - that 214
    % is not among the MONn routines the PLANC runtime provides, and that the
    % way round it is a MAC interface routine. That is the SAME reasoning that
    % was wrong about MONITOR_CALL by name, and wrong for the same reason: the
    % runtime's own MONn set is not the limit. MON-CALL-1B-A00 and its names
    % file are both linked into this client, and CHATSV has been calling
    % MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) - 267B - through them for weeks.
    %
    % GUARDED, like every monitor call here. An unguarded one that is refused
    % raises ROUTINEERROR and takes the whole program down - that is exactly how
    % this client died after joining. If the call is refused we simply keep
    % whatever name we already had.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : nameFromSintran
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : n
        BOOLEAN : failed

        FOR i IN 0:31 DO
            32 =: sintranUser(i)
        ENDFOR

        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: failed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: failed

        % Cleared first. Which of the five are IN and which are OUT is not clear
        % from the manual - the arrows beside the parameter list disagree with
        % the O in the overview table - so every one starts at a harmless value
        % and the MACHINE is allowed to settle it. The diagnostic below prints
        % what came back.
        0 =: dirIndex
        0 =: userIndex
        0 =: remoteFlag
        FOR i IN 0:63 DO
            32 =: remoteSystem(i)
        ENDFOR

        MONITOR_CALL('GetUserName', sintranUser, dirIndex, userIndex, &
            remoteFlag, remoteSystem)

        % Clear the handler before anything else can raise under it.
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
        ENDON

        % THE DIAGNOSTIC THAT LIVED HERE HAS DONE ITS JOB, and this is what it
        % said. Run on D100 2026-08-22 as user SYSTEM, old build against new:
        %
        %     @NDCHAT   ->  GetUserName refused        (the old ONE-argument call)
        %     @CHAT     ->  SINTRAN says: SYSTEM'      (the FIVE-argument call)
        %
        % So the call works and the five-parameter form was the whole problem.
        % Removed rather than left behind a flag - a diagnostic nobody can turn
        % off is noise on every single start-up.
        IF failed THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % THE ANSWER IS PADDED, AND NOT ONLY WITH SPACES. The name ends at the
        % first space, at a zero, or at an APOSTROPHE - the screen above shows
        % SYSTEM followed by one. Whether that apostrophe is SINTRAN's padding or
        % came from how the diagnostic printed the buffer was NOT established, so
        % all three are treated as terminators: an apostrophe cannot occur inside
        % a SINTRAN user name, so stopping at one is safe either way.
        %
        % Without this the join would carry padding inside the name field, and
        % two clients that "have the same name" would not match.
        0 =: n
        DO
            WHILE n < 31 AND sintranUser(n) >< 32 AND sintranUser(n) >< 0 &
                AND sintranUser(n) >< 39
            n + 1 =: n
        ENDDO

        % Remembered for readCnfg, which will not honour a saved nickname unless
        % it can prove the nickname belongs to whoever is logged in now.
        n =: lenSintranUser

        IF n = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % myName holds maxNameLen bytes and PLANC checks no array bound, so this
        % clamp is the only thing between a long user name and the variables
        % laid out after it.
        IF n > maxNameLen THEN
            maxNameLen =: n
        ENDIF

        FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
            sintranUser(i) =: myName(i)
        ENDFOR
        n =: lenMyName
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : setupName
        INTEGER : i

        % The fallback first, then whatever CHAT:CNFG remembers on top of it.
        % That way readCnfg can fail, or find an empty file, and simply leave a
        % good name in place - it never has to know what to fall back to, and
        % no path reaches the join with an empty name.
        %
        % THE ORDER IS MOST-SPECIFIC-LAST, each layer overwriting the one below:
        %   1. the built-in, which is only a shape to start from;
        %   2. WHO SINTRAN SAYS YOU ARE - you already logged in to tell it;
        %   3. a nickname you deliberately chose with /nick and that was saved.
        FOR i IN 1:lenDefault DO
            defaultName(i - 1) =: myName(i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        lenDefault =: lenMyName

        nameFromSintran

        readCnfg

        % NOTHING IS ANNOUNCED HERE, AND THAT IS THE FIX.
        %
        % This used to print "you are OLAV  (/nick to change)" the moment the
        % name was settled locally - BEFORE the join had been sent, let alone
        % answered. When the server then refused the name the screen read:
        %
        %     you are OLAV  (/nick to change)
        %     joining (LOBBY) - type and press return
        %     OLAV: that nickname is taken
        %
        % which states an identity and takes it away two lines later. A name we
        % merely INTEND to use is not an identity; only the server can grant it,
        % and only the WELCOME says it has. The announcement now lives where the
        % welcome is handled, so it is printed once and only when it is true.
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Point at a room, given its name - "LOBBY".
    %
    % THE PREFIX IS GONE, and with it the bug it existed to fix. This routine
    % used to paste "CHAT-" in front, because the room name WAS a port name and
    % the port is registered as CHAT-GENERAL rather than GENERAL. /join copied
    % the typed argument straight in, asked XROUT for a name that does not
    % exist, and the client stayed where it was - "/join doesn't work".
    %
    % There is no port name to build now. One port, *CHAT, and the room is
    % carried in the message, so the room name is just the room name. The whole
    % class of mistake goes with it.
    %
    % shortAt is an index into "typed"; a caller with the name somewhere else
    % copies it into typed first. That keeps this routine to one source and one
    % rule, which is the whole point of having it.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : setRoom (shortAt, shortLen)
        INTEGER : i

        FOR i IN 1:shortLen DO
            typed(shortAt + i - 1) =: roomName(i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        shortLen =: lenRoomName
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Say which room we are in, IN PARENTHESES.
    %
    % The client joins a room at start-up whether or not anybody asked it to,
    % and until now nothing on screen said which one - so every line you typed
    % went somewhere you had not been told about. The short name is shown, the
    % prefix left off: "CHAT-" is on every room and carries no information.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showRoom
        INTEGER : i

        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '(')
        FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
            roomName(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
        ENDFOR
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', ')')
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Send the join, as a LETTER through XROUT.
    %
    % The room is reached by NAME - XROUT looks it up and forwards, and this is
    % also where the seat is taken, before the server ever sees us. Everything
    % after the join goes port to port with no name lookup at all, which is why
    % the server's magic number is kept from its first reply.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % ------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Take a new nickname LOCALLY, from typed. No message goes anywhere.
    %
    % Used when a join was refused for a duplicate name: there is no room
    % to announce a rename to, so this just changes who we will say we are
    % the next time we knock.
    %
    % Bounded by maxNameLen, checked by the caller. PLANC checks no array
    % bound, so that check is the only thing between a long name and
    % whatever the compiler laid out after myName.
    % ------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : setMyName(nameAt, nameLen)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:nameLen DO
            typed(nameAt + i - 1) =: myName(i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        nameLen =: lenMyName
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Say who we are now. Called after a LOCAL rename, so the user can see the
    % name the next knock will use.
    %
    % THIS ROUTINE WAS CALLED AND NEVER EXISTED. The /nick recovery path has
    % read
    %
    %     setMyName(at, argLen)
    %     showMyName
    %     sendJoin
    %
    % since the day it was written, and there was no showMyName anywhere in the
    % file. PLANC answered
    %
    %     2494  (1834)/HANDLECOMM  *** ERROR   - ILLEGAL SYNTAX "SHOWMYNAME"
    %
    % every time - and nobody saw it, because the listing is the only place a
    % PLANC error survives and CHAT:LIST has never once been read. The MODE
    % file says so in as many words: "Not one *** ERROR in CHAT:LIST is the
    % only green light here." Every client build before 2026-08-21 went in
    % unchecked, so how much of the program the compiler skipped after this
    % point is NOT KNOWN.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showMyName
        INTEGER : i

        % JUST THE NAME, NO SENTENCE AROUND IT. This used to print a whole line -
        % "$CHAT: you are OLAV$" - which made it useless anywhere the name is
        % part of something else: a prompt, or "joining LOBBY as OLAV". A routine
        % called showMyName should show the name and nothing but; whoever wants a
        % sentence builds it.
        FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
            myName(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : sendJoin
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
        INTEGER : length

        % The XSLET header, built in a local buffer. 123 is our reference
        % number, echoed back so a task with several requests outstanding can
        % tell the answers apart.
        % A SUBARRAY, because roomName is now storage rather than a literal.
        %
        % XMPBLET takes portName as BYTES and appends it as parameter 1 of the
        % letter - a PLANC BYTES argument carries its own length, so handing it
        % the whole 21-byte array would ask XROUT for a name with eleven bytes
        % of leftovers on the end. roomName(0:lenRoomName-1) is exactly the name
        % and nothing else. Runtime bounds in a subarray are legal: the PLANC
        % manual's own example is ia1(0:10,i:k-2).
        % ADDRESSED TO THE SERVICE, NOT TO THE ROOM. The room is carried in the
        % message by buildJoin below.
        xmpblet(letterBuf, 64, offSet, 123, hereSystem, &
            servName(0:lenServName - 1)) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            showStatus('xmpblet', returnStatus)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        xmpfget(0, 200, msgIdent) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            showStatus('xmpfget', returnStatus)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % The header first...
        offSet =: uLength
        xmpfwri(0, 0, &
            ADDR(letterBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
            0, uLength, wLength) =: returnStatus

        % ...then OUR data after it. XROUT reads only the header and forwards
        % the rest untouched, so this is where the nickname rides.
        buildJoin =: length
        xmpfwri(0, wLength, &
            ADDR(outBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
            0, length, offSet) =: returnStatus

        xmprout(0, msgIdent, myPort) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            showStatus('xmprout (the join)', returnStatus)
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Send one of our messages straight to the server, no XROUT involved.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : sendToServer(length)
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : ident
        xmpfget(0, length, ident) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        xmpfwri(0, 0, &
            ADDR(outBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
            0, length, wLength) =: returnStatus
        xmpfsnd(0, myPort, serverMagic) =: returnStatus
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Show what arrived. The room speaks in the same message format we send.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % ===================================================================
    % THE SCREEN RENDERER
    %
    % Brought over from CHATUI.PLNC, which proved this layout on D100 on
    % 2026-08-25 before any of it went near the working client.
    %
    % WHAT WAS LEFT BEHIND, and it is the important half of this merge:
    % CHATUI invented a room name, a member count, a speaker list and a
    % five-second fake message feed, because it had no server behind it.
    % Every one of those is gone. The renderer now has to be given real
    % values or show nothing, which is the only way the example text in
    % the original mockup stops being example text.
    %
    % Also left behind: its own stack, its own spaceByte, and its own
    % input buffer - this client already has typed/typedLen, and reusing
    % them is what lets the whole command parser stay untouched.
    % ===================================================================


    CONSTANT bitEsc=1024
    CONSTANT bitCpos=2048
    CONSTANT bitVdu=16384

    % ---- THE GEOMETRY, ALL OF IT, IN ONE PLACE ---------------------------
    %
    % The three sections. Heights count rows INCLUSIVE, and the shared edges
    % are deliberate: topHigh ends on row 3 and midRow starts on row 3.
    CONSTANT topRow=1
    CONSTANT topHigh=3           % rows 1-3
    CONSTANT midRow=3
    CONSTANT midHigh=19          % rows 3-21
    CONSTANT botRow=21
    CONSTANT botHigh=4           % rows 21-24
    CONSTANT scrWide=80

    % Visible chat rows, in frame B's LOCAL coordinates. Frame B starts on
    % physical row 3, so its local row 2 is physical row 4.
    CONSTANT firstMsgRow=2       % local - physical row 4
    CONSTANT msgRows=17          % rows 4..20

    % A stored line is 77 characters covering physical columns 3..79. Frame B
    % starts at column 1, so local column = physical column and the line is
    % painted at column 3.
    %
    %   buffer 0..4    time      cols 3-7
    %   buffer 5..6    gap       cols 8-9
    %   buffer 7..19   speaker   cols 10-22
    %   buffer 20..76  text      cols 23-79
    CONSTANT lineWide=77
    CONSTANT textCol=3           % where a stored line is painted
    CONSTANT offTime=0
    CONSTANT offWho=7
    CONSTANT widWho=13
    CONSTANT offText=20
    CONSTANT widText=57

    % ---- THE SCROLLBACK CACHE --------------------------------------------
    %
    % A RING far larger than the window. Scrolling is one index change and no
    % text is copied; paging is a change to viewFrom and a repaint of the
    % middle section only - about 1300 characters, not the 2000 a whole screen
    % would cost.
    %
    % 300 lines x 77 = 23100 bytes, about 11550 words.
    %
    % IT WAS 100, WHICH WAS OVER-CAUTIOUS. The linker reported
    % FREE: P 066220-177777 after the whole program and all its libraries -
    % roughly 37000 words spare - so the cache can be three times the size and
    % still leave room. Ask the linker rather than guessing: the FREE line is
    % printed on every build and is the only number that matters.
    CONSTANT cacheLines=300
    BYTES : cache(0:23099)
    INTEGER : cacheCount := 0    % lines held, 0..cacheLines
    INTEGER : cacheFirst := 0    % slot holding the OLDEST line

    % HOW FAR BACK WE ARE LOOKING, counted in lines from the newest.
    % ZERO MEANS LIVE. Keeping it as a distance rather than an absolute slot
    % is what lets a new arrival leave it alone without any adjustment.
    INTEGER : viewFrom := 0

    % One line under construction.
    BYTES : lineBuf(0:76)
    INTEGER ARRAY : cal(0:6)

    % ---- THE INPUT LINE --------------------------------------------------
    % 76 columns, physical 4..79 on row 22.
    CONSTANT inpMax=76
    CONSTANT inpRow=2            % LOCAL row inside frame C - physical 22
    CONSTANT inpCol=4

    % DOES THIS ROOM REACH PAST THIS MACHINE?
    %
    % Set when a speaker arrives whose name carries an @ - which is the server
    % saying "this one is on another machine". Derived from real traffic, not
    % from a table this client would have to keep in step with the server's.
    %
    % It does not go back to FALSE on its own. A room that HAS been trunked is
    % still a trunked room when the other side goes quiet, and flickering the
    % marker off every idle minute would be worse than leaving it on.
    BOOLEAN : sawRemote := FALSE

    % ---- SINGLE CHARACTERS -----------------------------------------------
    % A STRING is not a BYTE in PLANC. 'x' =: buf(i) does not compile, and the
    % error names the ARRAY, which sends you to the declaration where nothing
    % is wrong.
    BYTES : digits := '0123456789'
    % spaceByte is NOT declared again here - the client already has one,
    % and a second draws IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED.
    BYTES : colonByte := ':'
    BYTES : starByte := '*'
    BYTES : atByte := '@'

    % MON3's echo table. Ignored for every strategy except 7; it exists
    % because the call takes three arguments.
    BOOLEAN ARRAY PACKED : echoTab(0:127)

    INTEGER4 : termType
    INTEGER4 : termKind

    % ---- WHO WE ARE, and the room --------------------------------------
    % PSEUDO-KEYS. Returned by pollKey for things that arrive as escape
    % sequences, so the main loop never has to know about them.
    % VTM'S OWN KEY CODES, MEASURED ON D100 2026-08-25 with KEYPROB.PLNC.
    % These are what VTINBT hands back after it has decoded whatever the
    % terminal actually sent, so they are the same on a TDV as on a VT100.
    %
    % Note the cursor keys are SMALL numbers, not high ones - so a rule like
    % "anything over 127 is a function key" would be wrong. They are ND
    % control codes, and a printable character is its ordinary ASCII value
    % with the parity bit already stripped by VTM.
    CONSTANT keyPgUp=201
    CONSTANT keyPgDn=197
    CONSTANT keyUp=28
    CONSTANT keyDown=11

    % =====================================================================
    % WINDOWS - VTM's own viewport. Absolute coordinates ALWAYS: VTDSCR does
    % not nest, and a second call reads its rectangle against the physical
    % screen rather than against the current one.
    % =====================================================================

    % TRUE only on a terminal that can address its cursor. Decided ONCE
    % at start-up from the CTYTP bits and never asked again - a renderer
    % that could change under the program would need every drawing site
    % to cope with both, which is how a screen ends up half-painted.
    %
    % FALSE for now. This increment brings the code across and proves it
    % compiles, links and fits; nothing calls it yet and the client
    % behaves exactly as it did before.
    % HOW MANY ARE IN THE ROOM. Zero until a /who answer has been counted -
    % CHATUI had this as the literal 14, which is exactly the kind of invented
    % number this merge removes. INTEGER4 because intdis takes a double word.
    INTEGER4 : hereCount := 0

    BOOLEAN : screenOn := FALSE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : winEnter(row, col, high, wide)
        INTEGER : st
        VTDSCR(row, col, row + high - 1, col + wide - 1) =: st
    ENDROUTINE

    % Back out to the whole screen. -1 is "to the end", the same convention
    % IVTCSCR(1,1,-1,-1) uses. VTINIT is not the only way back.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : winLeave
        INTEGER : st
        VTDSCR(1, 1, -1, -1) =: st
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : flush
        INTEGER : st
        VTWBUF =: st
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % SMALL HELPERS
    % =====================================================================

    % Two digits into a buffer at a given offset.
    %
    % CLAMPED INSIDE, not at the call sites. MN113's year element is the FULL
    % year - 1998, not 98 - and digits(1998/10) is digits(199), a read 190
    % bytes past the end of a ten-byte literal. PLANC checks no array bound,
    % so it fetches whatever follows and prints a blank where a digit belongs.
    % One clamp here covers every caller.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : put2(at, value)
        INTEGER : v
        value =: v
        IF v < 0 THEN
            0 =: v
        ENDIF
        v MOD 100 =: v
        digits(v / 10) =: lineBuf(at)
        digits(v MOD 10) =: lineBuf(at + 1)
    ENDROUTINE

    % Blank the whole line buffer. Every stored line is PADDED to full width
    % so a short line painted over a long one cannot leave the old tail
    % behind - that removes the need for a separate erase before each paint.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : blankLine
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 0:lineWide - 1 DO
            spaceByte(0) =: lineBuf(i)
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % Copy text into the line buffer at an offset, up to a field width, and
    % answer how many characters were taken. MAXINDEX asks the array its own
    % declared bound, so no length is ever typed beside a literal - a
    % hand-counted length builds clean and shows only as a line in the wrong
    % place.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES) : putField(at, wide, text)
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : n
        MAXINDEX(text, 1) + 1 =: n
        IF n > wide THEN
            wide =: n
        ENDIF
        IF n > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
                text(i) =: lineBuf(at + i)
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        n RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE SPEAKER COLUMN - 13 characters against a name that may be longer
    %
    % THE NAME ARRIVES ALREADY DECIDED. The server qualifies a speaker on
    % another machine as ANNA@D102 and leaves a local one bare, so this client
    % never has to work out which is which - and never has to know which
    % machine it is on, which it has no way of finding out.
    %
    % An earlier version of this DID decide for itself, from a nickname and a
    % system name it had been handed as two literals. That is exactly the kind
    % of invented state this merge exists to remove.
    %
    % What is left is the fitting rule, and it still matters: 13 columns
    % against a name that may be 16 plus @D102.
    %
    %   fits            copied as it is
    %   too long, no @  cut at 13
    %   too long, @     THE NAME IS CUT, NEVER THE MACHINE
    %
    % Cutting the machine would be the wrong choice: inside one room you know
    % a person from the first letters of their name, but D10 does not tell you
    % whether the line came from D100, D102 or D103 - and on a trunked room
    % that is the one thing this column is there for.
    % =====================================================================
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES) : putWho(who)
        INTEGER : total
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : bang
        INTEGER : tail
        INTEGER : room

        MAXINDEX(who, 1) + 1 =: total

        IF total <= widWho THEN
            FOR i IN 0:total - 1 DO
                who(i) =: lineBuf(offWho + i)
            ENDFOR
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % Where the machine starts, if there is one.
        0 - 1 =: bang
        FOR i IN 0:total - 1 DO
            IF bang < 0 THEN
                IF who(i) = atByte(0) THEN
                    i =: bang
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        IF bang < 0 THEN
            % No machine in it. Cut the name.
            FOR i IN 0:widWho - 1 DO
                who(i) =: lineBuf(offWho + i)
            ENDFOR
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % "@D102" and everything after it is kept whole; the name gets what is
        % left. If the machine alone fills the field there is no name shown -
        % which machine still beats a single initial.
        total - bang =: tail
        widWho - tail =: room
        IF room < 0 THEN
            0 =: room
        ENDIF

        IF room > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 0:room - 1 DO
                who(i) =: lineBuf(offWho + i)
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 0:tail - 1 DO
            IF room + i < widWho THEN
                who(bang + i) =: lineBuf(offWho + room + i)
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % EMPTY THE ROOM. Used when this client moves to another one.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : cacheClear
        0 =: cacheCount
        0 =: cacheFirst
        0 =: viewFrom
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : cacheAdd
        INTEGER : slot
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : base

        IF cacheCount >= cacheLines THEN
            % Full: the oldest slot is reused and becomes the newest.
            cacheFirst =: slot
            cacheFirst + 1 =: cacheFirst
            IF cacheFirst >= cacheLines THEN
                0 =: cacheFirst
            ENDIF
        ELSE
            cacheCount =: slot
            cacheCount + 1 =: cacheCount
        ENDIF

        slot * lineWide =: base
        FOR i IN 0:lineWide - 1 DO
            lineBuf(i) =: cache(base + i)
        ENDFOR

        % If the user is paged back, one more line has appeared BELOW them, so
        % the distance to the newest grows by one - otherwise the view would
        % drift forward a line every time somebody spoke.
        IF viewFrom > 0 THEN
            viewFrom + 1 =: viewFrom
            % ...but never past the oldest line we still hold.
            IF viewFrom > cacheCount - msgRows THEN
                cacheCount - msgRows =: viewFrom
            ENDIF
            IF viewFrom < 0 THEN
                0 =: viewFrom
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % Which cache slot holds the line at a given position, counted from the
    % OLDEST held line. The ring is unwound in ONE place, here.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : slotAt(index)
        INTEGER : s
        cacheFirst + index =: s
        IF s >= cacheLines THEN
            s - cacheLines =: s
        ENDIF
        s RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE ROOM'S MEMBERS, and the one question they answer
    % =====================================================================

    % =====================================================================
    % ADDING A LINE, WITH WRAPPING
    %
    % 57 columns of text is narrow and a real sentence overruns it. A line that
    % is simply CUT loses what somebody said, silently, which is the worst of
    % the three options.
    %
    % So a long line becomes SEVERAL cache lines: the first carries the time and
    % the speaker, and every continuation carries neither. That is what makes a
    % wrapped line readable as one utterance rather than as two people talking -
    % the eye follows the empty speaker column down.
    %
    % Each continuation is a cache line in its OWN right, so paging and the ring
    % need no special case at all. The cost is that a very long message uses
    % several of the 300 slots, which is correct: they ARE several screen lines.
    % =====================================================================

    % Put one row into the cache: a time and speaker only on the first.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, BYTES, BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            pushRow(withHead, who, text, from, count)
        INTEGER : i

        blankLine
        IF withHead = 1 THEN
            put2(offTime, cal(3))
            colonByte(0) =: lineBuf(offTime + 2)
            put2(offTime + 3, cal(2))
            putWho(who)
        ENDIF

        % The slice of the message this row carries. Copied byte by byte rather
        % than with a subarray, because the bounds are computed and a subarray
        % whose bounds fall outside the original breaks the compiler's own
        % index checking - appendix G item 22.
        IF count > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 0:count - 1 DO
                text(from + i) =: lineBuf(offText + i)
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        cacheAdd
    ENDROUTINE

    % Add what somebody said, wrapped across as many rows as it needs.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, BYTES) : addSaid(who, text)
        INTEGER : total
        INTEGER : at
        INTEGER : take
        INTEGER : head

        MN113(cal)

        % AN @ IN THE NAME IS THE SERVER SAYING "another machine", and that is
        % all the @trunk marker needs.
        FOR at IN 0:MAXINDEX(who, 1) DO
            IF who(at) = atByte(0) THEN
                TRUE =: sawRemote
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        MAXINDEX(text, 1) + 1 =: total
        0 =: at
        1 =: head

        % A DO loop with the test inside it, because PLANC has no while-header.
        % The first pass runs even for an empty message, so a blank line still
        % appears with its time - which is what a user expects if somebody
        % presses RETURN on nothing.
        DO
            total - at =: take
            IF take > widText THEN
                widText =: take
            ENDIF
            pushRow(head, who, text, at, take)
            0 =: head
            at + take =: at
            WHILE at < total
        ENDDO
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % PAINTING THE MIDDLE SECTION
    % =====================================================================

    % Repaint the chat. This is what a page costs and it is the most
    % expensive routine here: 17 lines of 77 characters, about 1300, roughly
    % a second and a half on a real 9600-baud line.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : paintChat
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : first
        INTEGER : shown
        INTEGER : idx
        INTEGER : base
        INTEGER : st

        winEnter(midRow, 1, midHigh, scrWide)
        VTCREC(firstMsgRow, textCol, firstMsgRow + msgRows - 1, textCol + lineWide - 1) =: st

        % Which held line goes on the top row. viewFrom counts BACK from the
        % newest, so at viewFrom = 0 the last msgRows lines are shown.
        cacheCount - msgRows - viewFrom =: first
        IF first < 0 THEN
            0 =: first
        ENDIF

        cacheCount - first =: shown
        IF shown > msgRows THEN
            msgRows =: shown
        ENDIF

        IF shown > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 0:shown - 1 DO
                slotAt(first + i) =: idx
                idx * lineWide =: base
                % THE WIDTH IS PASSED, NOT LEFT AS 0. Width 0 means "the
                % string's own length" and is right for a LITERAL; given a
                % SUBARRAY of a big buffer it ran off the right-hand side and
                % wiped borders a 77-character write could not reach.
                bytdis(firstMsgRow + i, textCol, lineWide, &
                       cache(base : base + lineWide - 1), '')
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        winLeave

        % SHOW IT. VTM holds its output in a buffer and a terminal READ is what
        % normally empties it, which is why every screen program that redraws
        % and then waits for a key appears to work without ever asking.
        %
        % This client does NOT wait for a key - it polls - so a repaint driven
        % by an arriving message has no read behind it and would sit in the
        % buffer. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26: a line said in a freshly joined
        % room stayed invisible until any keystroke, which reads exactly like a
        % lost message and is nothing of the kind.
        %
        % The callers flush too. Flushing an empty buffer costs nothing, and a
        % painter that leaves its work invisible is the trap this file has now
        % paid for twice.
        VTWBUF =: st
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE TOP SECTION
    % =====================================================================

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawStatus
        INTEGER : st
        INTEGER : at

        MN113(cal)

        winEnter(topRow, 1, topHigh, scrWide)
        VTCREC(2, 2, 2, scrWide - 1) =: st

        % ROW 2, laid out for the WORST CASE rather than for this room's name.
        % A room name may be sixteen characters and '@trunk' is six more, so
        % the name field can reach column 24 - which is why the count no longer
        % sits at 19. A layout measured against one example name is a layout
        % that breaks on the next one.
        %
        %   col  3..24   room name, and '@trunk' if it reaches another machine
        %   col 26..29   how many are here
        %   col 31..34   'here'
        %   col 37..58   the hint, OR the scrollback warning - never both
        %   col 64..74   the clock
        bytdis(2, 3, 0, roomName(0:lenRoomName - 1), '')
        % THE MARKER IS APPENDED TO THE NAME with no gap, as the design shows,
        % so it reads as part of the room's identity and not as another field.
        IF sawRemote THEN
            bytdis(2, 3 + lenRoomName, 0, '@trunk', '')
        ENDIF
        intdis(2, 26, 4, hereCount, '')
        bytdis(2, 31, 0, 'here', '')

        % The clock, into lineBuf because put2 works there.
        blankLine
        put2(0, cal(4))                    % day
        spaceByte(0) =: lineBuf(2)
        put2(3, cal(5))                    % month
        spaceByte(0) =: lineBuf(5)
        put2(6, cal(3))                    % hour
        colonByte(0) =: lineBuf(8)
        put2(9, cal(2))                    % minute
        bytdis(2, 64, 11, lineBuf(0:10), '')

        % PAGED BACK? Say so, because the arrival rule above means the screen
        % deliberately does not follow new lines while the user is reading.
        % Without this the client looks stuck.
        % THE HINT AND THE WARNING SHARE ONE SLOT, and that is deliberate.
        %
        % They used to sit side by side and the warning printed straight
        % THROUGH the hint - 'Use /help to lSCROLLBACKds' - which was patched
        % by moving it along until it fitted. Two things that are never both
        % worth reading should not both be drawn: the hint matters when nothing
        % is happening, and the moment the user has paged back, where they are
        % matters more than where the help is.
        IF viewFrom > 0 THEN
            bytdis(2, 37, 0, 'SCROLLBACK - PAGE DOWN ', '')
        ELSE
            bytdis(2, 37, 0, '/help for commands     ', '')
        ENDIF
        winLeave
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE BOTTOM SECTION
    % =====================================================================

    % Repaint the input line and leave the cursor at the end of it.
    %
    % THE CURSOR BELONGS HERE. In TESTUI it is parked somewhere harmless
    % because stray echoes landed inside windows; here there IS a right place
    % for it, so every repaint of anything must finish by calling this.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawInput
        INTEGER : st

        winEnter(botRow, 1, botHigh, scrWide)
        VTCREC(inpRow, 2, inpRow, scrWide - 1) =: st
        bytdis(inpRow, 2, 0, '>', '')
        IF typedLen > 0 THEN
            bytdis(inpRow, inpCol, typedLen, typed(0:typedLen - 1), '')
        ENDIF
        VTPCUR(inpRow, inpCol + typedLen) =: st
        winLeave
    ENDROUTINE

    % The window bar. DRAWN BUT INERT - multi-window is not built, and this
    % is here so the layout is proved with it in place rather than having to
    % find room for it later.
    % THE WINDOW BAR - one window, and it says which.
    %
    % It held a literal lifted straight out of the mockup: five room names and
    % two people who did not exist. That was worse than empty, because a
    % screen full of invented state looks finished when it is not.
    %
    % SO IT SHOWS WHAT IS TRUE, WHICH IS ONE WINDOW. There is exactly one
    % conversation - the room this client is in - and the bar names it. When
    % there are more, this is where they go; until then it is not pretending.
    %
    % The number is there because a switching key will need one, and a bar
    % that gains numbers later would move under the reader.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawBar
        INTEGER : at

        winEnter(botRow, 1, botHigh, scrWide)
        VTCREC(3, 2, 3, scrWide - 1) =: at

        blankLine
        0 =: at
        at + putField(at, 3, '1 ') =: at
        IF joined THEN
            at + putField(at, 20, roomName(0:lenRoomName - 1)) =: at
        ELSE
            % NOT IN A ROOM IS A REAL STATE and it says so, rather than
            % leaving a bare number with nothing after it.
            at + putField(at, 20, 'no room yet') =: at
        ENDIF

        bytdis(3, 4, at, lineBuf(0:at - 1), '')
        winLeave
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE WHOLE SCREEN - drawn ONCE
    % =====================================================================
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawAll
        INTEGER : at
        INTEGER : col

        % ONE BOX ROUND THE WHOLE SCREEN, then a rule across it for each
        % separator.
        %
        % THREE STACKED FRAMES WAS THE FIRST ATTEMPT AND IT LOOKED WRONG.
        % frame() draws a box, so where two of them touch you get the TOP
        % CORNERS of the lower box - a fresh box top sitting inside the
        % outer one - where the design wants a cross-piece. PLANC-SCREEN-H
        % has no T-junction verb, so the corners cannot be had that way.
        %
        % fullbar is the library's own horizontal rule and is what the
        % separators are for.
        winEnter(1, 1, 24, scrWide)
        frame(1, 1, 24, scrWide, 'SPACE-FILL')
        fullbar(midRow, 2, 1, scrWide - 2)
        fullbar(botRow, 2, 1, scrWide - 2)
        winLeave

        % The title is PAINTED OVER the top frame's own edge, which is what
        % the mockup shows. frame's fifth argument is ATTRIBUTES, not a title.
        bytdis(1, 4, 0, ' NDCHAT 2.1 ', '')
        % NAME@MACHINE - and the machine is the one the SERVER named in the
        % welcome, never one this client made up.
        %
        % CHATUI drew ARNE@D100 from two literals. This draws ARNE@FJELL, or
        % ARNE@D100 on a machine that has not been given a name, or ARNE alone
        % if the server never said - which is what an older server does, and
        % showing the name alone is the honest answer to not being told.
        blankLine
        0 =: at
        at + putField(at, widWho, myName(0:lenMyName - 1)) =: at
        IF lenMyMach > 0 THEN
            atByte(0) =: lineBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
            at + putField(at, 16, myMachine(0:lenMyMach - 1)) =: at
        ENDIF
        % RIGHT-ALIGNED so it always ENDS at column 77, just inside the frame.
        % A fixed start column was fine when this was the literal ARNE@D100 and
        % nine characters wide. A machine may now be called anything up to
        % sixteen, so a fixed start would run the corner straight through the
        % right-hand border on the first long name somebody chose.
        78 - at =: col
        IF col < 20 THEN
            20 =: col
        ENDIF
        bytdis(1, col, at, lineBuf(0:at - 1), '')

        drawStatus
        paintChat
        drawBar
        drawInput
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE FAKE FEED - one made-up line
    % =====================================================================
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : pageUp
        INTEGER : most
        cacheCount - msgRows =: most
        IF most < 0 THEN
            0 =: most
        ENDIF
        viewFrom + msgRows =: viewFrom
        IF viewFrom > most THEN
            most =: viewFrom
        ENDIF
        paintChat
        drawStatus
        drawInput
        flush
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : pageDown
        viewFrom - msgRows =: viewFrom
        IF viewFrom < 0 THEN
            0 =: viewFrom
        ENDIF
        paintChat
        drawStatus
        drawInput
        flush
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE KEYBOARD
    % =====================================================================

    % Does what has been typed start with this command word?
    %
    % CASE-FOLDED, because a user types /exit and /EXIT without thinking about
    % it, and SINTRAN itself does not care either.
    %
    % Compares against typedLen, so "/exits" does NOT match "/exit" - the length
    % has to be right as well as the letters. That matters the moment there is
    % a /e and a /exit to tell apart.
    ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (BYTES) : typedIs(word)
        INTEGER : n
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : a
        INTEGER : b

        MAXINDEX(word, 1) + 1 =: n
        IF n >< typedLen THEN
            FALSE RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF n = 0 THEN
            FALSE RETURN
        ENDIF

        FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
            typed(i) =: a
            word(i) =: b
            % Fold a lower-case letter up. 97 is 'a' and 122 is 'z'; 32 is the
            % gap to the capital.
            IF a >= 97 AND a <= 122 THEN
                a - 32 =: a
            ENDIF
            IF b >= 97 AND b <= 122 THEN
                b - 32 =: b
            ENDIF
            IF a >< b THEN
                FALSE RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        TRUE RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % Take one key WITHOUT waiting, and let VTM say what it was.
    %
    % TWO CALLS, AND EACH DOES THE HALF THE OTHER CANNOT.
    %
    %   MON66  answers HOW MANY BYTES ARE WAITING. That is what keeps the
    %          timer alive - without it the read blocks and nothing else in
    %          the program can happen.
    %   VTINBT reads the key and DECODES it. A function key is an escape
    %          sequence, every terminal sends a different one, and VTM is the
    %          layer that knows which. This program never sees a byte of it.
    %
    % A function key is several bytes on the wire and VTINBT consumes all of
    % them in one call, so MON66 answering "at least one" is enough - it does
    % not have to know how many the key will turn out to be.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : pollKey
        INTEGER : waiting
        % NOT "ch" - the client already has one and the compiler answers
        % IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED. It is only a warning, and that
        % is exactly why it is worth fixing rather than leaving: a shadowed
        % name compiles and then behaves in whichever way nobody expected.
        INTEGER : keyByte
        INTEGER : st
        INTEGER : dev
        INTEGER : mode
        INTEGER : arg4

        MON66(1) =: waiting
        IF waiting <= 0 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF

        % arg 1 is our own terminal. The 2 and the 3 are STAR-TREK's and are
        % NOT understood - no ND document describes this call at all - so they
        % are passed exactly as the vendor wrote them rather than guessed at.
        % PASSED AS VARIABLES, NOT LITERALS - and that is not fussiness.
        % MEASURED 2026-08-25: with literals in the call PAGE UP did nothing
        % at all, while the identical call with variables in KEYPROB.PLNC
        % returned 201 every time. The call is made the way it was proved.
        1 =: dev
        2 =: mode
        3 =: arg4
        0 =: keyByte
        VTINBT(dev, mode, keyByte, arg4) =: st
        keyByte RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % MAIN
    % =====================================================================

    % =====================================================================
    % THE SCREEN RENDERER'S SIDE OF AN ARRIVING MESSAGE
    %
    % One routine per JOB, not one per kind: everything the room says reduces
    % to a SPEAKER and a LINE, and the cache already knows how to wrap that
    % across rows. A notice with no speaker uses '*', which is what the line
    % renderer has always printed for the same thing.
    %
    % Answers TRUE when it has dealt with the message, so showArrived can
    % return and leave its OUTPUT code untouched. That is what lets the two
    % renderers live side by side instead of one being rewritten into the
    % other: the LINE renderer stays exactly as it was, and stays the fallback
    % for a printing terminal.
    % =====================================================================

    % A line being composed out of pieces. 250 rather than 256 because every
    % copy into it is clamped and the clamp needs somewhere to stop.
    BYTES   : uiBuf(0:255)
    INTEGER : uiLen := 0

    % THE SPEAKER GOES IN A BUFFER OF ITS OWN, and that is not tidiness.
    %
    % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26. addSaid was called as
    %
    %     addSaid(inBuf(2 : 2 + nameLength - 1), inBuf(textAt : textAt + textLength - 1))
    %
    % - TWO SUBARRAYS OF THE SAME ARRAY - and the rows came out smeared across
    % the screen, each one carrying part of its neighbour:
    %
    %     18:21  SYSTE@VIDDA
    %            SYSTE@VIDDASYSTEM@VIDDA!does this land without a keypress
    %                       SYSTEM@VIDDAsecond
    %
    % A NOTICE built the same line correctly the whole time, and that is what
    % identified it: uiNotice passes addSaid(starByte, uiBuf(...)) - two
    % DIFFERENT arrays - and rendered
    %
    %     18:21  *            here: TESTER SYSTEM@SKOGEN SYSTEM@VIDDA
    %
    % perfectly on the same screen, in the same paint, through the same
    % addSaid and the same pushRow. One variable differed.
    %
    % So the speaker is copied out first and the two arguments are never
    % subarrays of one array again.
    BYTES   : whoBuf(0:31)
    INTEGER : whoLen := 0

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : uiClear
        0 =: uiLen
    ENDROUTINE

    % Append a literal.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES) : uiWord(w)
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : n
        MAXINDEX(w, 1) + 1 =: n
        FOR i IN 1:n DO
            IF uiLen < 250 THEN
                w(i - 1) =: uiBuf(uiLen)
                uiLen + 1 =: uiLen
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % Append a field out of the arriving message.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : uiFrom(at, n)
        INTEGER : i
        IF n < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 1:n DO
            IF uiLen < 250 THEN
                inBuf(at + i - 1) =: uiBuf(uiLen)
                uiLen + 1 =: uiLen
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % A notice - no speaker, so the star column that the line renderer uses.
    % Append from a module buffer that carries its length separately - the
    % nickname and the machine both do. uiFrom reads inBuf; this does not.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER) : uiWho2(src, n)
        INTEGER : i
        IF n < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 1:n DO
            IF uiLen < 250 THEN
                src(i - 1) =: uiBuf(uiLen)
                uiLen + 1 =: uiLen
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % THE PANEL - an answer that is FOR YOU, not for the room
    %
    % /help, /who, /list, /map and an asked-for /topic are all replies to the
    % person who typed them. They are not things anybody said, so putting them
    % in the room mixes one person's questions into everybody's conversation
    % and pushes real lines off the top.
    %
    % They go in a window instead: built as a TABLE OF LINES, measured, and
    % centred over the room. Any key closes it.
    %
    % THE TABLE IS WHAT MAKES THE SIZING SAFE. The window measures the text,
    % so adding a command or lengthening a line moves the frame instead of
    % overflowing it. A window sized by a number typed next to the text goes
    % wrong the first time somebody edits the text - the same trap as a
    % hand-counted 'ALnn' width, which this file has already paid for.
    % =====================================================================
    CONSTANT panelMax=22
    CONSTANT panelWide=70
    BYTES : panelBuf(0:1539)
    INTEGER ARRAY : panelLen(0:21)
    INTEGER : panelRows := 0

    % TRUE while the window is up. Two things read it: the key loop, which
    % closes the panel instead of typing into the input line, and the repaint,
    % which must NOT redraw the room over the top of it.
    BOOLEAN : panelUp := FALSE

    % DID THIS CLIENT ASK? Both of these answers arrive as a kind that is also
    % used for something else - kWho for the count taken automatically on the
    % way in, kTopic for a change the whole room is told about - so the kind
    % alone cannot say whether the reply is FOR YOU. Typing the command is
    % what makes it yours.
    % HAS THE FIRST WELCOME BEEN SEEN? Every /join gets a welcome of its own,
    % so without this the "you are TESTER on FJELL" line is repeated on every
    % room change - and the machine has not changed, so it says nothing new.
    BOOLEAN : firstWelcome := TRUE

    BOOLEAN : askedWho := FALSE
    BOOLEAN : askedTopic := FALSE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : panelClear
        0 =: panelRows
    ENDROUTINE

    % One line from a literal.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES) : panelAdd(t)
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : n
        INTEGER : base

        IF panelRows >= panelMax THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        MAXINDEX(t, 1) + 1 =: n
        IF n > panelWide THEN
            panelWide =: n
        ENDIF
        panelRows * panelWide =: base
        IF n > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 1:n DO
                t(i - 1) =: panelBuf(base + i - 1)
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        n =: panelLen(panelRows)
        panelRows + 1 =: panelRows
    ENDROUTINE

    % A run of text out of the arriving message, WRAPPED onto as many lines as
    % it needs.
    %
    % IT BREAKS ON A SPACE WHERE IT CAN. A /who answer is a list of names and
    % cutting one in half mid-name is exactly the thing a reader has to undo
    % in their head. Where there is no space to break on - one enormous word -
    % it cuts, because the alternative is a line that runs off the frame.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : panelAddFrom(at, n)
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : from
        INTEGER : take
        INTEGER : look
        INTEGER : cut
        INTEGER : base

        IF n < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        0 =: from
        DO
            WHILE from < n
            IF panelRows >= panelMax THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            n - from =: take
            IF take > 60 THEN
                60 =: take

                % Walk back to the last space in what we were going to take.
                0 =: cut
                FOR i IN 1:take - 1 DO
                    IF inBuf(at + from + i) = spaceByte(0) THEN
                        i =: cut
                    ENDIF
                ENDFOR
                IF cut > 0 THEN
                    cut =: take
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            panelRows * panelWide =: base
            0 =: look
            FOR i IN 1:take DO
                IF look < panelWide THEN
                    inBuf(at + from + i - 1) =: panelBuf(base + look)
                    look + 1 =: look
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
            look =: panelLen(panelRows)
            panelRows + 1 =: panelRows

            from + take =: from

            % Skip the space we broke on, so the next line does not start with
            % it and look indented by one.
            IF from < n THEN
                IF inBuf(at + from) = spaceByte(0) THEN
                    from + 1 =: from
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDDO
    ENDROUTINE

    % The command list. NOT the room name - the status line already carries
    % that, and it was the one part of the old help that was not constant.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : buildHelp
        panelClear
        panelAdd('CHAT commands')
        panelAdd(' ')
        panelAdd('  /join <room>          join another room')
        panelAdd('  /nick <name>          change your nickname')
        panelAdd('  /who                  who is in this room')
        panelAdd('  /list                 rooms, and how many in each')
        panelAdd('  /map                  the machines and their trunks')
        panelAdd('  /topic                show the room topic')
        panelAdd('  /topic <text>         set it')
        panelAdd('  /tell <name> <text>   a message to one person')
        panelAdd('  /leave                leave the room')
        panelAdd('  /exit                 Exit chat program')
        panelAdd(' ')
        panelAdd('  anything else is said to the room')
    ENDROUTINE

    % =====================================================================
    % DRAW IT - measured from the table, centred over the room
    % =====================================================================
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawPanel
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : w
        INTEGER : h
        INTEGER : row
        INTEGER : col
        INTEGER : base

        panelAdd(' ')
        panelAdd('  -- any key closes this --')

        % THE WIDEST LINE DECIDES THE WIDTH. Nothing is counted by hand, and
        % the closing hint above is measured with everything else - it is a
        % line like any other, so it cannot be the one that overflows.
        0 =: w
        IF panelRows > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 0:panelRows - 1 DO
                IF panelLen(i) > w THEN
                    panelLen(i) =: w
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF

        % Two columns of frame and one space of padding either side.
        w + 4 =: w
        panelRows + 2 =: h

        % CLAMPED TO THE SCREEN. Text taller than the terminal would otherwise
        % draw a frame off the bottom and take the layout with it. Clamping
        % loses the tail, which is visible and fixable; drawing past the edge
        % is neither.
        IF w > scrWide THEN
            scrWide =: w
        ENDIF
        IF h > 24 THEN
            24 =: h
        ENDIF

        % CENTRED. Integer division floors, so an odd remainder puts the
        % window one column left of centre rather than off the edge.
        (scrWide - w) / 2 + 1 =: col
        (24 - h) / 2 + 1 =: row
        IF col < 1 THEN
            1 =: col
        ENDIF
        IF row < 1 THEN
            1 =: row
        ENDIF

        % A VIEWPORT CLIPS, so everything below is in the panel's own
        % coordinates and nothing it draws can reach the room behind it.
        winEnter(row, col, h, w)
        frame(1, 1, h, w, 'SPACE-FILL')
        IF panelRows > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 0:panelRows - 1 DO
                i * panelWide =: base
                IF panelLen(i) > 0 THEN
                    IF 2 + i < h THEN
                        bytdis(2 + i, 3, panelLen(i), &
                               panelBuf(base : base + panelLen(i) - 1), '')
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        winLeave
        TRUE =: panelUp
    ENDROUTINE

    % An answer that arrived as a message: a heading, then the text wrapped.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : panelAnswer(head, at, n)
        panelClear
        panelAdd(head)
        panelAdd(' ')
        IF n < 1 THEN
            panelAdd('  (nothing)')
        ELSE
            panelAddFrom(at, n)
        ENDIF
        drawPanel
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : uiNotice
        IF uiLen < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        addSaid(starByte, uiBuf(0:uiLen - 1))
    ENDROUTINE

    % Copy the speaker out of the arriving message into its own buffer.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : uiWho(at, n)
        INTEGER : i
        0 =: whoLen
        IF n < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 1:n DO
            IF whoLen < 30 THEN
                inBuf(at + i - 1) =: whoBuf(whoLen)
                whoLen + 1 =: whoLen
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % A line somebody said: speaker in one buffer, text in another.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            uiSaid(nameAt, nameLen, txtAt, txtLen)
        uiWho(nameAt, nameLen)
        uiClear
        uiFrom(txtAt, txtLen)
        IF whoLen < 1 THEN
            uiNotice
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF uiLen < 1 THEN
            addSaid(whoBuf(0:whoLen - 1), spaceByte)
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        addSaid(whoBuf(0:whoLen - 1), uiBuf(0:uiLen - 1))
    ENDROUTINE


    % HOW MANY NAMES ARE IN A /who ANSWER.
    %
    % The server sends them space separated - "TESTER SYSTEM@SKOGEN
    % SYSTEM@VIDDA" - so counting the names is counting the runs of non-space.
    % Nothing is parsed beyond that: the client does not care what a name looks
    % like, only how many there are.
    %
    % IT COUNTS PEOPLE ON OTHER MACHINES TOO, which is what "here" should mean
    % in a room that spans machines - the same answer /who itself gives.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : countNames(at, n)
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : seen
        INTEGER : inWord

        0 =: seen
        0 =: inWord
        IF n < 1 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
            IF inBuf(at + i) = spaceByte(0) THEN
                0 =: inWord
            ELSE
                IF inWord = 0 THEN
                    seen + 1 =: seen
                ENDIF
                1 =: inWord
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        seen RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN : uiArrived
        INTEGER : kind
        INTEGER : nameLength
        INTEGER : textLength
        INTEGER : textAt

        IF NOT screenOn THEN
            FALSE RETURN
        ENDIF

        inBuf(0) =: kind
        inBuf(1) =: nameLength

        % THE SAME LENGTH GUARD THE LINE RENDERER USES, and for the same
        % reason: nameLength is one byte and can say 255, which would read
        % past the end of a message that never held that much.
        IF 2 + nameLength + 1 > readLength THEN
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF
        2 + nameLength =: textAt
        inBuf(textAt) * 256 + inBuf(textAt + 1) =: textLength
        textAt + 2 =: textAt

        % ---- somebody spoke -------------------------------------------
        % A ZERO-LENGTH FIELD WOULD MAKE A REVERSED SUBARRAY - inBuf(2:1) -
        % and PLANC checks no bounds, so it would be handed whatever that
        % happens to mean. Both are built as a notice instead, which is also
        % the more useful thing to show.
        IF kind = kSaid THEN
            uiSaid(2, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        % REPLAYED HISTORY is drawn exactly like live traffic here, and that
        % is a deliberate difference from the line renderer, which marks it
        % with a bar. On a screen the lines above the newest ARE the past, so
        % the mark says nothing the position does not.
        IF kind = kHist THEN
            uiSaid(2, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- a direct message, and it stays marked ---------------------
        %
        % The stars are not decoration. A reply typed as an ordinary line goes
        % to the whole room, so a private message that looks like room traffic
        % is a privacy failure caused entirely by the display.
        IF kind = kDirected THEN
            uiClear
            uiWord('*(')
            uiFrom(2, nameLength)
            uiWord(') ')
            uiFrom(textAt, textLength)
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kDirSent THEN
            uiClear
            uiWord('sent to ')
            uiFrom(2, nameLength)
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kDirBad THEN
            uiClear
            uiWord('NOT sent to ')
            uiFrom(2, nameLength)
            uiWord(': ')
            uiFrom(textAt, textLength)
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- who came and went -----------------------------------------
        IF kind = kJoined THEN
            % ONE MORE, WITHOUT ASKING. Asking the server on every arrival
            % would put a letter on the wire for every join in the room, and
            % the next /who corrects any drift anyway.
            hereCount + 1 =: hereCount
            uiClear
            uiFrom(2, nameLength)
            uiWord(' has joined')
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kLeft THEN
            % ONE FEWER, AND NEVER BELOW ZERO. A leave can arrive for somebody
            % this client never saw join - it joined after them - and counting
            % down past zero would show a negative in the status line.
            IF hereCount > 0 THEN
                hereCount - 1 =: hereCount
            ENDIF
            uiClear
            uiFrom(2, nameLength)
            uiWord(' has left')
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        % A RENAME CARRIES THE NEW NAME IN THE NAME FIELD and the old one as
        % the text - the order that is easiest to get backwards, which is why
        % the C# golden test pins it.
        IF kind = kRenamed THEN
            uiClear
            uiFrom(textAt, textLength)
            uiWord(' is now ')
            uiFrom(2, nameLength)
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- the room itself -------------------------------------------
        %
        % The TOPIC answer names the ROOM in the name field where a nickname
        % normally sits, so the name is not printed as a speaker here.
        IF kind = kTopic THEN
            % ONE KIND, TWO MEANINGS, and only this client knows which it is
            % looking at. An ASKED-FOR topic is an answer to a question nobody
            % else heard; a topic somebody SET is news for the whole room. The
            % wire cannot tell them apart - the server sends the same kind - so
            % the flag set when /topic was typed with no argument is what
            % decides, and it is cleared whichever way this goes.
            IF askedTopic THEN
                FALSE =: askedTopic
                IF textLength > 0 THEN
                    panelAnswer('the topic', textAt, textLength)
                ELSE
                    panelClear
                    panelAdd('the topic')
                    panelAdd(' ')
                    panelAdd('  none set')
                    drawPanel
                ENDIF
                TRUE RETURN
            ENDIF

            uiClear
            uiWord('topic: ')
            IF textLength > 0 THEN
                uiFrom(textAt, textLength)
            ELSE
                uiWord('none set')
            ENDIF
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- answers to a question, all one shape ----------------------
        %
        % who / everybody / rooms / map are readable text built by the server
        % and are not parsed by anything here. They wrap across rows exactly
        % as a long sentence does.
        IF kind = kWho THEN
            % THE COUNT IS TAKEN FROM THE ANSWER, not kept by this client.
            % Whatever the server says is right, and it is right again every
            % time somebody asks - so a count that has drifted repairs itself
            % rather than needing to be got right for ever.
            countNames(textAt, textLength) =: hereCount

            % THE ONE THIS CLIENT ASKED FOR GOES IN A PANEL; the one it asked
            % for ITSELF on the way in does not. Without that test the screen
            % would open a window over the room the moment you joined, which
            % nobody asked for and which would have to be dismissed before the
            % first line could be read.
            IF askedWho THEN
                FALSE =: askedWho
                panelAnswer('who is here', textAt, textLength)
            ENDIF
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kAllWho THEN
            FALSE =: askedWho
            panelAnswer('everybody, every room', textAt, textLength)
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kRooms THEN
            panelAnswer('rooms', textAt, textLength)
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kMap THEN
            panelAnswer('the machines', textAt, textLength)
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kReject THEN
            uiClear
            uiWord('refused: ')
            uiFrom(textAt, textLength)
            uiNotice
            TRUE RETURN
        ENDIF

        % kWelcome is NOT taken here. It sets serverMagic, joined and the
        % machine name, and all of that lives in showArrived - so it falls
        % through on purpose and the line renderer's own branch runs. Its
        % printing goes to a screen that is about to be drawn over, which
        % costs nothing.
        %
        % ANY OTHER KIND ALSO FALLS THROUGH, which is the right default: an
        % unknown kind reaching the line renderer prints something a person
        % can report, and reaching this one would vanish in silence.
        FALSE RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showArrived
        INTEGER : kind, nameLength, textLength, textAt, i
        % Only the screen path uses this - the /who it asks for on the way in.
        INTEGER : length
        % Only used by the kRenamed branch: is this rename about US?
        BOOLEAN : sameAsOurs
        INTEGER : newLen

        % ON A SCREEN TERMINAL THE RENDERER TAKES IT FIRST.
        %
        % uiArrived answers FALSE when it has not dealt with the message -
        % which is every kind on a printing terminal, and the welcome and any
        % unknown kind on a screen one - and then everything below runs exactly
        % as it always has. THE LINE RENDERER IS NOT TOUCHED BY THIS, which is
        % what makes it safe: it stays the reference for what should be shown
        % and the fallback for a terminal that cannot address its cursor.
        IF uiArrived THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        inBuf(0) =: kind
        inBuf(1) =: nameLength

        % THE JOIN IS ONE STEP, and a two-step handshake used to live here.
        %
        % It was added on the reading that "sending the join through XROUT does
        % not put the nickname in front of the server" - that the server's
        % connection port is handed a bare 4-byte connect, so a kHello reply was
        % needed before the nickname could be sent. That reading was WRONG, and
        % it was wrong because the SERVER was reading the wrong bytes: a letter
        % arrives with its XSLET header still attached, and the server was
        % parsing the header's serial number as a message kind.
        %
        % The nickname was in the first letter the whole time. sendJoin writes it
        % after the header and the Programmer Guide is explicit that XROUT
        % forwards it untouched. The server's WELCOME is the answer that hands
        % over the magic number, which is all this side ever needed. See the
        % header skip in CHATSV.PLNC for the layout and the manual reference.
        %
        % Nothing replaces this block: the welcome below already sets
        % serverMagic, and always did.

        % ---- TRUST NOTHING THAT ARRIVED, ON THIS SIDE TOO ------------------
        % The server checks exactly these three things before it reads a name or
        % a text; this side did not, and the reasoning is identical. inBuf holds
        % 256 bytes and PLANC checks no array bound:
        %
        %   nameLength is ONE BYTE, so it can say 255. The name loop would then
        %   read inBuf(2)..inBuf(256) - past the end - and textAt would already
        %   be 257 before any text was touched.
        %
        %   textLength is TWO BYTES, so it can say 65535, and the text loop
        %   would walk tens of thousands of bytes off the end of the array.
        %
        %   A SHORT message is the quieter half: without testing against
        %   readLength the loops print whatever the previous, longer message
        %   left behind in inBuf.
        %
        % "The server would never send that" is not a reason. A truncated
        % message does it by accident, and a client that only survives a
        % well-behaved peer is not one worth writing.
        % Both tests are against readLength, which the read below clamps to the
        % size of inBuf - so a field that fits the MESSAGE also fits the ARRAY.
        IF 2 + nameLength + 1 > readLength THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        2 + nameLength =: textAt
        inBuf(textAt) * 256 + inBuf(textAt + 1) =: textLength
        textAt + 2 =: textAt

        IF textAt + textLength > readLength THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % The WELCOME is what reveals the server's address: everything after it
        % goes straight there.
        IF kind = kWelcome THEN
            senderMagic =: serverMagic
            TRUE =: joined

            % THE WELCOME'S TEXT IS THE MACHINE THIS CLIENT IS ON. It was
            % always empty before, so a server that has not been upgraded
            % simply leaves this empty and the screen shows the name alone.
            IF textLength > 0 THEN
                textLength =: lenMyMach
                IF lenMyMach > 16 THEN
                    16 =: lenMyMach
                ENDIF
                FOR i IN 1:lenMyMach DO
                    inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: myMachine(i - 1)
                ENDFOR

                % REDRAW THE WHOLE FRAME, because the corner is painted by
                % drawAll and drawAll ran before this arrived - the join is
                % sent first and the welcome answers it. Without this the
                % corner says TESTER for the rest of the session on a machine
                % that has a perfectly good name.
                %
                % Once per session, so the cost does not matter.
                IF screenOn THEN
                    drawAll
                    flush
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            % ASK WHO IS HERE, ONCE, so the status line starts out TRUE.
            %
            % The welcome is the first moment there is a seat to ask from. Only
            % on a screen: the line renderer has no status line to fill, and an
            % unasked-for /who answer printed at a user who did not type one
            % would be noise.
            IF screenOn THEN
                buildOwn(kWho, 0) =: length
                sendToServer(length)
            ENDIF

            % SAY WHO YOU ARE, ONCE, AND ONLY NOW THAT IT IS TRUE. The server
            % has accepted the name, so this is a fact rather than a hope - see
            % the note in setupName for what printing it early looked like.
            % ON A SCREEN THIS GOES IN THE ROOM, NOT TO THE CURSOR.
            %
            % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26: printed with OUTPUT while the frame
            % was already up, it landed ON THE INPUT ROW -
            %
            %     > you are TESTER on FJELL  (/nick to change)
            %
            % because OUTPUT writes wherever the cursor happens to be, and the
            % cursor is parked on the input line by drawInput. It used to be
            % harmless: the welcome arrived before the first paint and was
            % simply drawn over. It is not harmless now that the welcome also
            % triggers a repaint.
            %
            % The corner already says TESTER@FJELL, so this is only the
            % sentence - and a sentence belongs with the other sentences.
            IF screenOn THEN
                IF firstWelcome THEN
                    FALSE =: firstWelcome
                    uiClear
                    uiWord('you are ')
                    uiWho2(myName, lenMyName)
                    IF lenMyMach > 0 THEN
                        uiWord(' on ')
                        uiWho2(myMachine, lenMyMach)
                    ENDIF
                    uiNotice
                ELSE
                    % A LATER WELCOME IS A ROOM CHANGE, and the lines above
                    % belong to the room just left. Keeping them would put one
                    % room's conversation under another room's name in the
                    % status line and the bar - which is worse than losing
                    % them, because it is not visibly wrong.
                    %
                    % They ARE lost, and that is the honest cost of one buffer
                    % for one window. A buffer per window is what fixes it, and
                    % that is the next phase rather than something to fake now.
                    cacheClear

                    % AND PAINT THE EMPTY ROOM AT ONCE.
                    %
                    % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26: without this, the FIRST
                    % line said in the new room was drawn into VTM's buffer
                    % and stayed there - invisible - until the next terminal
                    % READ flushed it. One keystroke, any keystroke, made it
                    % appear. So it looked like the say had been lost, or the
                    % client had gone deaf after a join, when in fact the
                    % renderer had already done its work and nobody had asked
                    % VTM to show it.
                    %
                    % A room change is rare, so a whole repaint here costs
                    % nothing and leaves the screen in a state that agrees
                    % with the buffer before the first new line arrives.
                    drawAll
                    flush
                ENDIF
            ELSE
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', 'you are ')
                showMyName

                % AND WHICH MACHINE, when the server said. Without it the fact
                % would be invisible on a printing terminal - a feature you
                % cannot see is a feature you cannot test.
                IF lenMyMach > 0 THEN
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL', ' on ')
                    FOR i IN 1:lenMyMach DO
                        myMachine(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
                    ENDFOR
                ENDIF

                OUTPUT(1, 'AL20', '  (/nick to change)$')
            ENDIF

            % RETURN, LIKE EVERY OTHER CASE IN THIS CHAIN.
            %
            % This branch had no RETURN, so after printing the welcome it walked
            % on through every remaining test and fell into the printer at the
            % bottom - which printed the sender's name and the welcome's empty
            % text as
            %
            %     SYSTEM:
            %
            % That bare line was read as screen noise for two sittings, and the
            % kJoined/kLeft branches were added on the theory that it was an
            % arrival notice. It was not: it was THIS message, arriving once and
            % being printed twice.
            %
            % IT WAS THE "unknown message kind: 2" DIAGNOSTIC THAT EXPOSED IT, on
            % the first run after that diagnostic was added - kind 2 is kWelcome,
            % a kind which plainly HAS a case, and a handled kind reaching the
            % bottom of the chain can only mean the case did not stop. Making the
            % silent path loud paid for itself immediately.
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- OUR OWN NAME CHANGES ONLY WHEN THE ROOM SAYS SO ---------------
        % A comment in handleCommand has promised this for as long as /nick has
        % existed - "our own name changes only when the room says so, with
        % kRenamed" - and nothing implemented it. The name in myName therefore
        % stayed at the old one after a successful rename, so the next thing
        % this client SENT went out under a name the room no longer knew.
        %
        % A Renamed carries the NEW name in the name field and the OLD one as
        % its text, which is exactly what is needed to tell whether it is about
        % us: compare the OLD name with what we currently believe we are called.
        % Everybody in the room gets every Renamed, so without that test this
        % client would take somebody else's new name for itself.
        %
        % This is also the ONLY place CHAT:CNFG is written. Saving when /nick is
        % SENT would remember a name the server may be about to refuse - it
        % refuses an empty one, and one already taken - so the save waits for
        % the room's confirmation. A refused rename changes nothing, on screen
        % or on disk.
        IF kind = kRenamed THEN
            IF textLength = lenMyName THEN
                TRUE =: sameAsOurs
                FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                    IF inBuf(textAt + i - 1) >< myName(i - 1) THEN
                        FALSE =: sameAsOurs
                    ENDIF
                ENDFOR

                IF sameAsOurs THEN
                    % Bounded by the buffer, not by what arrived. A server that
                    % sent a longer name than we can hold would otherwise write
                    % past the end of myName - and the room's own limit and
                    % this buffer are both sixteen, so a name that does not fit
                    % here is one the room should never have accepted.
                    nameLength =: newLen
                    IF newLen > maxNameLen THEN
                        maxNameLen =: newLen
                    ENDIF
                    FOR i IN 1:newLen DO
                        inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: myName(i - 1)
                    ENDFOR
                    newLen =: lenMyName

                    writeCnfg
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            % RETURN, for the same reason kWelcome now does. This branch had none
            % either, so a rename would have printed nothing of its own and then
            % fallen through to the bottom of the chain to be rendered as speech.
            % Two of the seven cases were missing it; a check that every branch
            % ends in RETURN is worth running whenever a case is added.
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % A BYTE HANDED TO 'AL1' PRINTS AS A NUMBER, NOT A CHARACTER.
        %
        % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-18, the first run in which the join actually
        % completed: the welcome arrived, was received and released correctly,
        % and printed as
        %
        %     8279787889: 7269767679
        %
        % which is 82 79 78 78 89 = RONNY and 72 69 76 76 79 = HELLO, each byte
        % rendered as its decimal value. The message was perfect; only the
        % printing was wrong, and it looked alarming enough to be mistaken for
        % a decode fault.
        %
        % 'ALn' formats a STRING. inBuf(x) is a BYTE, so it takes the integer
        % path. Copying the byte into a one-character BYTES first gives 'AL1'
        % the string it is asking for. This uses nothing new - BYTES arrays,
        % assignment and OUTPUT with ALn on a BYTES all appear above and all
        % compile.
        % THE /who ANSWER IS NOT A LINE SOMEBODY SAID, so it does not get the
        % "$NAME: text" shape below. Its name field is empty on purpose - the
        % room is answering, not a member - and the generic printer would open
        % with a bare colon. Printed here and returned.
        IF kind = kWho THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL10', '$CHAT: in ')
            showRoom
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', ': ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % THE TOPIC. Its NAME field carries the ROOM, not a person - it is the
        % one message that is about a place - so it cannot use the speech
        % printer either, and it must not print the room as if somebody with
        % that name had spoken.
        %
        % An EMPTY text is not a blank topic, it is NO topic. Saying so in
        % words beats printing "topic:" followed by nothing, which reads like
        % something that failed to arrive.
        IF kind = kTopic THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL13', '$CHAT: topic ')
            % 2, not a nameAt local - showArrived has no such variable and the
            % name field starts at index 2 in every message. Writing nameAt here
            % drew "2465 (1805)/SHOWARRIVE *** ERROR - ILLEGAL SYNTAX", which is
            % the compiler naming an undeclared identifier rather than a syntax
            % problem. Compare the kReject branch below, which has always used
            % inBuf(2 + i - 1).
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            IF textLength = 0 THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', ' has no topic yet$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', ': ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % THE ROOM LIST. Empty name, like /who - the server is answering, not
        % a member. The text is already "LOBBY(2) GENERAL(1)".
        % /who WHEN YOU ARE IN NO ROOM - every room and the people in each.
        %
        % A SEPARATE KIND FROM kWho, and the branch above shows exactly why: it
        % prints "CHAT: in (LOBBY): ..." using the room THIS CLIENT thinks it is
        % in. Printing every room's people under that label would state
        % something false - the same defect /who itself had until it learned to
        % compare rooms. So this one carries no room label at all.
        %
        % NOTHING IS PARSED. The server sends it ready to read - rooms separated
        % by two spaces, people by one - and a format nobody parses cannot be
        % parsed wrongly.
        %
        % AN EMPTY TEXT IS A REAL ANSWER: nobody is anywhere. Said in words,
        % because a bare "CHAT: everyone " reads like something that failed to
        % arrive - the same reason kTopic spells out an absent topic.
        IF kind = kAllWho THEN
            IF textLength = 0 THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', '$CHAT: nobody is in any room$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL16', '$CHAT: everyone ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % REPLAYED HISTORY, and it is MARKED so it cannot be mistaken for
        % somebody talking now.
        %
        % This is the whole reason the server sends a distinct kind instead of
        % replaying kSaid. Printed like speech but with a leading marker, so a
        % transcript reads:
        %
        %     | OLAV: what did you think of it
        %     | ANNA: not much
        %     OLAV joined
        %     ANNA: hello again
        %
        % - everything before the join visibly older than everything after.
        IF kind = kHist THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL3', '$| ')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', ': ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /map's answer. Printed as it stands, like the rooms list and the
        % all-rooms /who: the server sends it ready to read and the client
        % parses nothing.
        IF kind = kMap THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL11', '$CHAT: map ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kRooms THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL13', '$CHAT: rooms ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % A REFUSAL IS NOT SPEECH, AND MUST NOT BE PRINTED AS SPEECH.
        %
        % kReject carries the name that was REFUSED and the reason for it. There
        % was no case for it here, so it fell through to the "<name>: <text>"
        % printer below and came out as
        %
        %     OLAV: that nickname is taken
        %
        % which reads as OLAV saying those words in the room. He did not: the
        % server said them ABOUT him. The name belongs where it is the SUBJECT,
        % not in the slot reserved for whoever spoke, and the message has to say
        % what to do next - a refusal the user cannot act on is just a wall.
        IF kind = kReject THEN
            % TWO REFUSALS ARRIVE HERE AND THEY MEAN DIFFERENT THINGS.
            %
            % If the user has NOT chosen a name this session, we joined under the
            % name SINTRAN gave us - so "taken" means the SAME SINTRAN USER is
            % already in the room from ANOTHER TERMINAL. That is worth saying in
            % those words, because "that nickname is taken" sends someone hunting
            % for a stranger who has stolen their name, when in fact it is their
            % own second window.
            %
            % DECIDED 2026-08-22: refuse the second instance and say so plainly.
            % Not auto-suffixed to SYSTEM-2, and not two sessions sharing one
            % identity - both of those make identity per-INSTANCE, which the
            % trunk would then have to keep distinct across machines.
            %
            % The user is NOT thrown out: /nick still works, so a second window
            % can join under a name of its own if that is what was wanted.
            IF NOT userChoseNick THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                    inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
                ENDFOR
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL37', ' is already in the room from another$')
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL39', 'terminal - use /nick <name> to join as$')
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL14', 'somebody else$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL15', 'cannot join as ')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL3', ' - ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'use /nick <name> to pick another$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- ARRIVALS AND DEPARTURES ARE EVENTS, NOT SPEECH -------------------
        %
        % These two had NO case at all and fell through to the "<name>: <text>"
        % printer below, exactly as kReject once did. A join carries no text, so
        % it came out as a bare
        %
        %     SYSTEM:
        %
        % which reads as somebody typing a colon and nothing else - screen noise
        % rather than a bug, which is why it sat there unnoticed. It was seen on
        % 2026-08-22 straight after a /nick and dismissed twice before the
        % dispatch chain was actually read.
        %
        % THE NAME IS THE SUBJECT HERE, not the speaker, so it goes in front of a
        % verb and never in front of a colon.
        IF kind = kJoined THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', ' joined$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kLeft THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL6', ' left$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- SPEECH, AND ONLY SPEECH ------------------------------------------
        %
        % THE TEST IS EXPLICIT NOW, and that is the whole point. This used to be a
        % bare fall-through that printed WHATEVER was left as "<name>: <text>", so
        % every kind nobody had written a case for arrived here and was rendered
        % as a person talking. Three separate bugs came out of that one habit:
        % kReject as "OLAV: that nickname is taken", kJoined as a bare "SYSTEM:",
        % and - the same blindness one layer up - writeCnfg hung off kRenamed
        % while the server answers kWelcome, so /nick was confirmed and never
        % saved.
        %
        % A KIND NOBODY HANDLES MUST BE LOUD. It produces no error from the
        % compiler and none at run time, so the only way it ever gets noticed is
        % if the program says so itself. tools/planc-lint.py now flags a kind that
        % is declared and never used again, which would have caught all three
        % before they reached the machine.
        % A DIRECT MESSAGE, ADDRESSED TO ME PERSONALLY.
        %
        % MARKED SO IT CANNOT BE MISTAKEN FOR ROOM TRAFFIC, and that is not
        % decoration: a reply typed as an ordinary line would go to the whole
        % room, which is a privacy failure caused entirely by the display.
        % The stars say it was private, and the name is printed QUALIFIED -
        % D102!KARI - because that is exactly what has to be typed back.
        IF kind = kDirected THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', '*(')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', ') ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % DELIVERED, AND HERE IS WHO TO.
        %
        % Shown on EVERY direct message and not only the ambiguous ones. The
        % server refuses an ambiguous name outright, which protects you when
        % the collision is visible; this protects you when it is not - a second
        % RONNY who logged in a moment ago, a machine that just went down. One
        % line is a cheap price for a wrong delivery being visible at once
        % rather than days later.
        IF kind = kDirSent THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'sent to: ')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % NOT DELIVERED, AND HERE IS WHY.
        %
        % The reason is printed as it arrived. For a name that means more than
        % one person it is the CANDIDATE LIST, which is what makes the refusal
        % usable: the sender retypes one of them straight off the screen.
        IF kind = kDirBad THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'not sent to ')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', ': ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kSaid THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
                inBuf(2 + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', ': ')
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL23', 'unknown message kind: $')
        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', kind)
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')

        % AND THE FIRST BYTES, BECAUSE THE NUMBER ALONE CANNOT SETTLE IT.
        %
        % MEASURED 2026-08-22: kind 123 arrives on EVERY start-up, and the
        % welcome line that should be there is MISSING - so 123 IS the welcome,
        % mis-read, not an extra message. The same welcome parses correctly when
        % it arrives LATER (on the /nick path), and both paths call the same
        % drainPort. So the difference is ordinal: the FIRST message on the port
        % is read differently from every one after it.
        %
        % 123 is 0x7B, '{' - a printable character sitting where a kind belongs,
        % which is what a BODY byte looks like when every field has been shifted
        % by an unskipped header. CHATSV carries a comment about exactly this
        % class of fault on the SERVER side ("serial number instead of kJoin,
        % which is how the missing header skip..."), so this may be its twin.
        %
        % These six bytes decide it. If they read as text or as the tail of a
        % letter header, the shift is confirmed and the fix is a header skip on
        % the first receive. If they look like a sane message that simply has an
        % unexpected kind, the answer is somewhere else entirely.
        %
        % TEMPORARY. Delete once the question is answered - a dump nobody can
        % turn off is noise, which is the mistake the status line already made.
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL12', 'first six: $')
        FOR i IN 0:5 DO
            OUTPUT(1, 'I5', inBuf(i))
        ENDFOR
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Take everything the port is holding and print it, WITHOUT WAITING.
    %
    % flags ZERO means no XFWTF, and that is XMSG's own no-wait: the call
    % returns at once whether or not a message was there. Nothing about the
    % terminal is involved, which is why this works on a machine where the
    % no-wait TERMINAL switch (TNOWAIT 307B) cannot be reached from PLANC.
    %
    % A SUCCESSFUL RECEIVE DOES RETURN ZERO ON THIS MACHINE, and the opposite
    % claim used to stand here. It read: "A SUCCESSFUL RECEIVE DOES NOT RETURN
    % ZERO ... a message arrived exactly when the status is POSITIVE", citing
    % the constants file's "Message types: Returned as successfull status from
    % XFRCV", XMTNO=1 through XMTPS=6.
    %
    % MEASURED 2026-08-18: the server, doing the same call, prints st= 0 with
    % the type in the msgType out-parameter. The constants file is describing
    % the RAW MON 200B interface underneath; through the XMP library the status
    % is XMOK and the type comes back separately. Appendix G's sample agrees -
    % it tests "IF RETURNSTATUS >< XMOK" and only then reads MSGTYPE.
    %
    % Excluding zero cost the whole join: XMPFRCV took the welcome off the
    % queue, returned XMOK, and the test below dropped straight out of the loop
    % without printing, reading or releasing it. See the note on the test.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % PLANC HAS NO "WHILE cond DO ... ENDWHILE". WHILE is a CONTINUE-TEST
    % written INSIDE a DO ... ENDDO body: reaching it with a false condition
    % leaves the loop. ND-60.117.5 shows it that way ("continue the loop only
    % for negative array elements ... WHILE a(i)<0"), and writing the header
    % form gets WHILE NOT INSIDE LOOP followed by ILLEGAL SYNTAX "ENDWHILE".
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Print an XMSG status with its name and meaning, then say where it came
    % from. Called on every path that gives up, so a failure never leaves just
    % a number - or worse, nothing at all.
    %
    % THE LIBRARY'S STATUS MODEL IS NOT THE KERNEL'S, and that difference cost
    % a whole evening. Through the XMP routines:
    %
    %   0                  XMOK - done. The kernel's own OK is 1; XMP-B02:DEFS
    %                      remaps it and says so: "NOTE change from XMSG OK=1".
    %   16896              XMXENTM - NOT TERMINATED. This is what an EMPTY port
    %                      answers, and it is NOT an error.
    %   16896 + n          an error, where n is the absolute value of the raw
    %                      kernel code. So the raw XEIBP = -6 appears here as
    %                      XMXEIBP = 16902.
    %   1 .. 6             a MESSAGE TYPE. Documented as a status from XFRCV,
    %                      but MEASURED 2026-08-18 the library answers XMOK and
    %                      puts the type in the msgType out-parameter instead.
    %                      Both forms are accepted on a receive for that reason.
    %
    % Which is why testing a receive with "> 0" is wrong: 16896 and every error
    % are positive too. Measured live: type=0 rel=16902, an empty port being
    % read as a message and then released, for ever.
    %
    % AND WHY TESTING IT WITH "1..6 ONLY" IS EQUALLY WRONG, which took longer to
    % find because it fails SILENTLY: XMPFRCV dequeues the message and returns
    % XMOK, the test rejects it, and the loop exits having printed nothing. A
    % receive must accept XMOK **or** 1..6 - see drainPort.
    %
    % The numbers are from XMP-B02:DEFS as extracted from the ND-10609 media;
    % the texts are the matching XE... comments in XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Switch this terminal's INPUT side in and out of no-wait.
    %
    % THIS IS THE THING THE POLL LOOP ALWAYS ASSUMED AND NOBODY EVER SWITCHED
    % ON. The loop below was written for a no-wait terminal - it catches
    % ROUTINEERROR from MON1 and reads ErrCode 3 as "nothing was typed" - and a
    % comment there claims "the terminal is in no-wait". It was not. Nothing in
    % this program ever asked for it, so MON1 BLOCKED, and the loop never got
    % back round to drainPort until the user pressed return.
    %
    % MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-20: two people in a room, and neither saw the
    % other's line until they typed something themselves. The speaker always saw
    % their own words at once, because pressing return is what let the loop go
    % round. That is indistinguishable from a message that was never sent, and
    % it cost an afternoon of hunting a delivery fault in a server that was
    % delivering to everybody correctly.
    %
    % IT DID NOT NEED MAC AFTER ALL. The notes at the foot of this file weigh a
    % hand-written MAC interface routine for TNOWAIT (307B) against an XFWAK
    % experiment, because "TNOWAIT cannot be had here". Both are unnecessary:
    % MONITOR_CALL takes a call BY NAME and handles output parameters - CHATSV
    % has been calling MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) for exactly that reason -
    % and MON-CALL-1B-A00 is already in this client's LOAD list. NoWaitSwitch
    % (NOWT 36B) takes three parameters and ALL THREE ARE INPUTS, so there is
    % not even an output to get wrong.
    %
    % ALWAYS SWITCH IT BACK. A terminal left in no-wait is one the user's shell
    % has to live with after this program ends, so every exit path goes through
    % terminalWait - see the leave path at the end of the main loop.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % EVERY ONE OF THESE IS GUARDED, AND THE FIRST VERSION WAS NOT.
    %
    % MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-21. The unguarded call killed the client the
    % instant it joined:
    %
    %     @NDCHAT
    %     CHAT: you are OLAV  (/nick to change)
    %     CHAT: joining (LOBBY) - type and press return
    %     NO ROUTINEERROR HANDLER, ERRETURN=177777B
    %     @
    %
    % A MONITOR_CALL that SINTRAN refuses raises a ROUTINEERROR, and an
    % unhandled one unwinds the whole program - after the join has been sent,
    % so it also leaves a seat behind in the room. The server was fine; the
    % client shot itself.
    %
    % THE FALLBACK IS THE OLD BEHAVIOUR, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS SAFE. Without the
    % switch, MON1 blocks and the loop comes round when the user presses return
    % - the very thing this change is trying to improve, but a working client.
    % So a refusal is worth ONE LINE ON SCREEN and nothing more.
    %
    % WHICH NAME IS RIGHT IS NOT SETTLED. MONITOR_CALL by NAME works here -
    % CHATSV has been calling MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) for weeks - so the
    % mechanism is proved and it is the SPELLING that is in doubt. The Monitor
    % Calls manual gives two candidates and their short names:
    %     NoWaitSwitch   (NOWT   36B)  LDN, io flag, no-wait flag - ALL INPUTS
    %     TerminalNoWait (TNOWAI 307B) LDN, io flag, no-wait flag, status OUT
    % If the line below prints, try the other name, and then try inputFlag the
    % other way round - 0 for input is what this file has always claimed and
    % nothing has ever exercised it.
    % TWO NAMES TRIED, AND THE REASON PRINTED. One build, one answer.
    %
    % 'NoWaitSwitch' is refused on D100 - measured 2026-08-21, the client
    % printed its fallback line. But a refusal alone does not say WHETHER the
    % name is unknown or the PARAMETERS are wrong, and guessing between those
    % costs a build cycle each time. ErrCode is supplied by the runtime inside
    % the handler and it is the difference between the two, so it is printed.
    %
    % Then the other spelling is tried. The Monitor Calls manual offers both:
    %     NoWaitSwitch   (NOWT   36B)  LDN, io flag, no-wait flag       3 IN
    %     TerminalNoWait (TNOWAI 307B) LDN, io flag, no-wait flag, st   3 IN 1 OUT
    % MONITOR_CALL handles an output parameter - CHATSV passes one to TimeOut -
    % so the second form costs nothing to attempt.
    %
    % IF BOTH ARE REFUSED, the remaining suspect is ownTerminal. It is 1, the
    % SINTRAN "own terminal" file number, and what NoWaitSwitch wants is the
    % LDN OF A CHARACTER DEVICE, which is not obviously the same thing.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % ASK SINTRAN WHICH TERMINAL THIS IS.
    %
    % ExecutionInfo (RSIO 143B) hands back the execution mode and, for an
    % interactive program, "your terminal number" as BOTH the command input and
    % the command output device. That is the LDN the no-wait calls want, and the
    % manual names this call by name as the way to get it.
    %
    % ALL FOUR PARAMETERS ARE OUTPUTS. Nothing goes in.
    %
    % The mode is worth having anyway: 0 interactive, 1 batch, 2 mode job,
    % 3 RT. A chat client run from a MODE file gets a FILE number here, not a
    % terminal, and switching no-wait on that would be meaningless - so it is
    % only used when the mode says interactive.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : findMyLdn
        BOOLEAN : askFailed
        INTEGER : execMode, inDev, outDev, userIdx

        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: askFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: askFailed

        0 =: execMode
        0 =: inDev
        0 =: outDev
        0 =: userIdx

        MONITOR_CALL('ExecutionInfo', execMode, inDev, outDev, userIdx)

        % Interactive only, and only if it gave back something usable. Anything
        % else leaves myLdn at 1, which is the behaviour this program has always
        % had - a failure here must not make the client worse than it was.
        IF NOT askFailed THEN
            IF execMode = 0 THEN
                IF inDev > 1 THEN
                    inDev =: myLdn
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : terminalNoWait
        BOOLEAN : switchFailed
        INTEGER : nowaitStatus

        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: switchFailed
            ErrCode =: firstErr
        ENDON
        FALSE =: switchFailed
        0 =: firstErr

        % THE NAME THAT WORKS GOES FIRST, and this used to be the other way
        % round. NoWaitSwitch is REFUSED on this machine - ErrCode -1, measured -
        % so trying it first meant printing
        %
        %     CHAT: NoWaitSwitch err     -1
        %
        % on EVERY start-up, before quietly succeeding under the other name. A
        % diagnostic for a call we already know will fail is not a diagnostic,
        % it is noise, and it made a working start-up look broken. terminalWait
        % has always tried TerminalNoWait first; the two must agree or the
        % restore does nothing.
        0 =: nowaitStatus
        % myLdn, NOT ownTerminal. See the long note beside the declaration:
        % ownTerminal is a FILE number and the manual says file numbers are
        % illegal here. That single substitution is the whole fix.
        MONITOR_CALL('TerminalNoWait', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOn, &
            nowaitStatus)

        IF switchFailed THEN
            % Fall back SILENTLY. A machine where the other name is the live
            % one is a possibility, not a fault worth reporting.
            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                TRUE =: switchFailed
                ErrCode =: firstErr
            ENDON
            FALSE =: switchFailed
            0 =: firstErr

            MONITOR_CALL('NoWaitSwitch', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOn)
        ENDIF

        % ONLY IF BOTH WERE REFUSED is anything said, and then it says what the
        % user will SEE rather than which call failed - the number helps nobody
        % holding a keyboard.
        % A REFUSAL ARRIVES TWO DIFFERENT WAYS, AND BOTH MEAN THE SAME THING TO
        % WHOEVER IS HOLDING THE KEYBOARD.
        %
        %   NoWaitSwitch   (NOWT   36B)  RAISES          - ErrCode -1
        %   TerminalNoWait (TNOWAI 307B) RAISES NOTHING  - status -1
        %
        % MEASURED ON D100 2026-08-22 by printing the status, which until then
        % was captured into nowaitStatus and NEVER READ. That is why this was a
        % puzzle for so long: the call looked like it worked, because it did not
        % raise, and MON1 went on blocking anyway. AN ABSENT ERROR IS NOT A
        % SUCCESS - the earlier note here claimed TerminalNoWait "SUCCEEDS", and
        % that was inferred from the absence of a raise rather than measured.
        %
        % THAT CONCLUSION WAS WRONG, AND IT IS KEPT ABOVE BECAUSE IT IS THE
        % INSTRUCTIVE PART. "No-wait terminal input is simply NOT AVAILABLE to
        % this program on this machine" was written down as a fact to design
        % around. It was a MEASUREMENT of one wrong argument, promoted to a
        % property of the machine.
        %
        % Status -1 is defined in ND-860228-2 as "error in parameters", and the
        % offending parameter was the first one: a FILE number where an LDN
        % belongs. The manual says so beside every call that takes an LDN, and
        % names ExecutionInfo as the way to get the right value. See findMyLdn.
        %
        % TWO WHOLE DAYS went into the SPELLING of the call and the value of the
        % io flag - the two arguments that were already correct - because the
        % status number was read as a verdict on the call instead of as what it
        % literally says: your parameters are wrong.
        %
        % The number IS printed again if it still fails, with the LDN beside it,
        % because those two together are the whole diagnosis. One line, only on
        % failure, and it should never appear.
        % SAY IT WHATEVER THE STATUS SAYS, because the status is now LYING to
        % the user by being 0. This line used to fire on a refusal, and a
        % refusal is exactly what we no longer get - the call is accepted and
        % the behaviour is unchanged. Tying the warning to the status would
        % leave a user with a client that silently sits on their messages and
        % nothing on screen to explain it, which is worse than the old noise.
        %
        % Put it back under the IF the day a silent listener sees a line arrive
        % on its own. That is the test; nothing else counts.
        %
        % THAT DAY IS HERE FOR THE SCREEN RENDERER, and only for it. Its
        % keyboard asks MON66 how many bytes are waiting instead of trusting
        % no-wait, so the loop goes round without a keystroke and a line DOES
        % arrive on its own. The warning would be false, and it was printing
        % over the window bar as well - it is written for a renderer that owns
        % the whole page and scrolls it.
        %
        % The LINE renderer still means every word of it.
        IF NOT screenOn THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', '$new lines show when you press return$')
        ENDIF

        % THE MEASUREMENT THAT ANSWERED IT, AND IT IS NOT GOOD NEWS.
        %
        % A build printing the LDN and the status unconditionally was run on
        % D100 on 2026-08-22. It said:
        %
        %     CHAT: no-wait ldn     38 status      0
        %
        % 38 is a REAL terminal LDN - the console banner for that line ends
        % "TERMINAL 7/ TET10 L:46 (38)" and the 38 in brackets is it. So
        % ExecutionInfo worked, the LDN is right, the io flag is right (the
        % manual: 0 for input), and the call is ACCEPTED - status 0, where the
        % same call with file number 1 had answered -1.
        %
        % AND MON1 STILL BLOCKS. Two clients, one left untouched: the welcome,
        % a joining notice and a line spoken from the other terminal all sat
        % queued and arrived together on one keystroke, exactly as before.
        %
        % So the parameters were genuinely wrong AND fixing them was not enough.
        % What remains unexplained is why an accepted no-wait switch changes
        % nothing about InByte on this machine. UNVERIFIED, and the honest state
        % of it: a plain SINTRAN call, accepted, with no observable effect.
        % Suspect the emulated terminal driver before suspecting the program -
        % but suspect nothing until it is measured, because guessing at this
        % call has already cost two days.
        %
        % The print is gone again: it fires on every start-up and says nothing
        % a user can act on. The numbers are here instead, with a date.
    ENDROUTINE

    % Putting it BACK is silent whatever happens. If the switch never went on,
    % switching it off is a no-op; if it did, this is the only thing standing
    % between the user and a shell whose terminal does not wait for input.
    % PUT IT BACK WITH THE NAME THAT ACTUALLY WORKS.
    %
    % This used to call NoWaitSwitch, which is REFUSED on this machine with
    % ErrCode -1 - so the restore silently did nothing while the switch-on had
    % quietly succeeded under the other name. A terminal left in no-wait is one
    % the user's shell has to live with, so the two must use the SAME name.
    % TerminalNoWait is tried first here for that reason, with NoWaitSwitch kept
    % only as a fallback for a machine where the other one is the live one.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : terminalWait
        BOOLEAN : switchFailed
        INTEGER : nowaitStatus

        0 =: nowaitStatus
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: switchFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: switchFailed

        MONITOR_CALL('TerminalNoWait', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOff, &
            nowaitStatus)

        IF switchFailed THEN
            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                TRUE =: switchFailed
            ENDON
            FALSE =: switchFailed
            MONITOR_CALL('NoWaitSwitch', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOff)
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % Give the machine back a moment. Called only when a pass found NOTHING -
    % no message waiting and no key pressed - so it never delays anything that
    % actually happened. Guarded for the same reason as the two above: a sleep
    % that cannot be had is not a reason to end the program.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Give the machine a moment when a pass found nothing.
    %
    % A GUARD THAT SETS A FLAG NOBODY READS IS NOT A GUARD, IT IS A SILENCER.
    % This routine set sleepFailed and never looked at it again, so a refused
    % TimeOut returned instantly and the main loop went round again with NO
    % SLEEP AT ALL - a SPIN against XMSG, invisible from the screen, the log and
    % the kernel tables alike. The comment beside sleepUnits already warns that
    % a bare loop here is the shape that burned the machine under CHATSV.
    %
    % That is exactly how GetUserName hid: guarded, refused, silent, and only
    % found because a diagnostic line was added on purpose. Every swallowed
    % failure in this program is a place the machine can be hurt without
    % anything saying so.
    %
    % SAID ONCE, NOT EVERY PASS. At a fifth of a second a per-pass message is a
    % flood that hides itself, so the first refusal speaks and the rest are
    % counted by their absence. If this line appears, the client has NO pacing
    % and should not be left running.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : idleSleep
        BOOLEAN : sleepFailed
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: sleepFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: sleepFailed

        MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', sleepUnits, sleepKind, sleepWhy)

        IF sleepFailed AND NOT sleepBroken THEN
            TRUE =: sleepBroken
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL43', '$WARNING: cannot sleep - this client SPINS$')
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drainPort
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
        INTEGER : taken

        % THIS LOOP IS BOUNDED, and that is not caution for its own sake - the
        % unbounded version hung the client on the first live run. It kept being
        % handed a message, printed it, and asked again for ever; the terminal
        % filled with the same line until ESC broke it. Whether the message was
        % never really released or the peer kept re-offering it is not settled,
        % but either way a loop that only ends when the PEER says so is a loop
        % the peer can hang. One pass takes at most this many and then returns
        % to the keyboard; anything still waiting is taken on the next pass.
        0 =: taken

        DO
            xmpfrcv(0, myPort, msgType, remotePortHash, msgIdent, nBytes) &
                =: returnStatus

            % XMOK IS A SUCCESSFUL RECEIVE. THIS TEST MUST ACCEPT ZERO.
            %
            % It did not, and that single missing case is what silently ate the
            % welcome. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-18, end to end:
            %
            %   the server prints  SV: rcv st=  0  t=  2  len= 25
            %                                  ^ XMOK, with the type in msgType
            %
            %   the client's port  LIST-PORTS: port 13  Qhead 161641  Qlen 1
            %   one keystroke later                     Qhead 0       Qlen 0
            %   and the client printed NOTHING - not even the unconditional
            %   "type= / rel=" line below, which every taken message reaches.
            %
            % That is the whole story: XMPFRCV succeeded, returned XMOK, and
            % DEQUEUED the message - which is why Qlen fell to zero - and then
            % this continue-test read 0 >= XMTNO as false and left the loop
            % before a single byte was printed, before XMPFMST, before XMPFREA,
            % and before XMPFREL. The message was received and abandoned. It was
            % never returned to the sender either, which is why the server saw
            % no bounce.
            %
            % THE OLD COMMENT HERE ARGUED THE OPPOSITE and was wrong. It claimed
            % "a successful receive does not return zero", on the reasoning that
            % 0 means OK, 16896 means an empty port, and a bare "> 0" test is
            % true for both - all of which is correct ABOUT THE OTHER CALLS and
            % does not follow for XMPFRCV. The vendor sample in Appendix G tests
            % "IF RETURNSTATUS >< XMOK THEN bad status" and only then looks at
            % MSGTYPE, so XMOK is the success case and the TYPE lives in the
            % out-parameter, not in the status.
            %
            % The earlier "type=0 rel=16902" reading that seemed to prove the
            % old rule was an EMPTY port being read and then released - a real
            % fault, but a different one, and fixing it by excluding zero threw
            % out every genuine message with it.
            %
            % CHATSV was widened for exactly this reason and this client never
            % was. Both sides now spell the same rule, and the range is kept
            % beside XMOK rather than replaced by it, because which form turns
            % up is measured rather than assumed.
            %
            % 16896 (XMXENTM, "not terminated") is what an EMPTY port answers
            % and is still correctly excluded - it is neither XMOK nor 1..6.
            WHILE returnStatus = XMOK OR &
                  (returnStatus >= XMTNO AND returnStatus <= XMTPS)

            taken + 1 =: taken
            WHILE taken <= maxDrain

            xmpfmst(0, msgIdent, msgType, senderMagic, nBytes) =: returnStatus

            % CLAMP BEFORE READING. nBytes is the SENDER'S message size, handed
            % back by XMSG - not a size this program chose. inBuf holds
            % inBufSize bytes and PLANC checks no array bound, so passing nBytes
            % straight in lets anyone who sends a longer message write straight
            % past the end of it. That is a WRITE overflow and it happens before
            % a single field is parsed, so none of the checks in showArrived
            % could save it.
            nBytes =: readAmount
            IF readAmount > inBufSize THEN
                inBufSize =: readAmount
            ENDIF

            xmpfrea(0, 0, &
                ADDR(inBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
                0, readAmount, readLength) =: returnStatus
            % A RETURN MESSAGE IS OUR OWN LETTER COMING BACK UNDELIVERED.
            % XMTRE is the type XMSG uses for "abnormal condition", and the
            % commonest reason a join bounces is that the peer does not have us
            % as a friend - the same fault XRUNN and XRNRO report from the
            % other direction. Saying so here means the user is told once,
            % whichever way the machine chooses to complain.
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL40', 'that message came BACK - undeliverable.$')
                showFriendAdvice
            ENDIF

            xmpfrel(0, msgIdent) =: returnStatus

            % A "type= rel=" line used to print here on every message taken.
            % It was temporary, it said so, and it has done its job: the loop
            % that printed the same message for ever is understood and fixed
            % (see the ROUTINEERROR handler in the main loop), and the empty
            % port being read and released - the "type=0 rel=16902" reading -
            % is excluded by the continue-test above.
            %
            % What it proved is kept in the comments that surround it rather
            % than on the user's screen. Put it back if this loop ever misbehaves
            % again: msgType and returnStatus are the two numbers that say why.

            showArrived
        ENDDO

        % How many this pass printed, for the main loop's idle test. Nothing
        % else reads it, and it is a COUNT rather than a boolean so a future
        % question like "are we falling behind" has a number to look at.
        taken =: lastDrainCount

        % ---- DID THE PORT GO AWAY WHILE WE WERE NOT LOOKING? ---------------
        % The loop above ends on ANY status that is not a message, and until now
        % it ended silently on all of them. That is right for XMXENTM, which
        % just means the port is empty and is the normal way round; it is wrong
        % for XMXENRU, "XMSG is not running", because the client then sits at
        % its prompt looking perfectly connected to a room that no longer
        % exists, and everything typed goes nowhere.
        %
        % MEASURED 2026-08-18: XMSG went down under a running server. The
        % SERVER span at full speed on the same status (it waits with XFWTF, so
        % a receive that cannot block returns instantly for ever); this client
        % would not have spun, because its drain never waits - it would just
        % have gone quiet and told nobody. Two different failures from one
        % cause, which is why both programs needed their own answer.
        %
        % Stopping is the honest one here. There is no reconnect to attempt: a
        % port belongs to an XMSG that is gone, and when it comes back this
        % program has to open a new one and rejoin from the beginning - which
        % is exactly what starting it again does.
        IF returnStatus = XMXENRU THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XMSG has stopped - leaving$')
            FALSE =: joined
            0 =: serverMagic
            FALSE =: running
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Does the typed line begin with "/word"? Case is folded, so /WHO and /who
    % are the same command.
    %
    % typed(0) is the slash, so the word starts at 1. A match also requires the
    % character AFTER the word to be a space or the end of the line - without
    % that test "/nickname" would match "/nick" and the rest be read as an
    % argument.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER) : cmdIs(word, wordLen)
        INTEGER : i, c, w

        IF typedLen < wordLen + 1 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF

        FOR i IN 1:wordLen DO
            typed(i) =: c
            % Fold lower case to upper: 'a' is 97, 'z' is 122.
            IF c >= 97 AND c <= 122 THEN
                c - 32 =: c
            ENDIF
            word(i - 1) =: w
            IF c >< w THEN
                0 RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        % The word must END here - the next character is a space or nothing.
        IF typedLen = wordLen + 1 THEN
            1 RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF typed(wordLen + 1) = 32 THEN
            1 RETURN
        ENDIF

        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % A line beginning with "/" is a command, not something to say.
    %
    % WHAT IS AND IS NOT IMPLEMENTED, and the difference matters. /nick, /leave
    % and /quit map onto message kinds that ALREADY EXIST on the wire - kRename
    % and kLeave - and /join re-sends the join CHAT already sends at startup, to
    % a different room name. None of those invent anything.
    %
    % /who does NOT map onto anything. There is no query kind in the format, and
    % the format is pinned byte-for-byte by ChatMessageGoldenBytesTests on the
    % C# side, so adding one is a change to BOTH ends in the same commit - not
    % something this file may do on its own. It says so rather than pretending.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : handleCommand
        INTEGER : at, argLen, i, length
        % Where the name ends and the message begins in a /tell.
        INTEGER : cut

        IF cmdIs('HELP', 4) = 1 THEN
            % ON A SCREEN THE HELP IS A WINDOW, and it is built from the same
            % table the line renderer prints below - so the two can never say
            % different things, and the window is measured from the text
            % rather than sized by a number somebody has to remember to update.
            IF screenOn THEN
                buildHelp
                drawPanel
                flush
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            OUTPUT(1, 'AL15', 'CHAT commands:$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL11', 'you are in ')
            showRoom
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL47', '  /join <room>   join another room, short name$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', '  /nick <name>   change your nickname$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL37', '  /who           who is in this room$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL45', '  /list          rooms, and how many in each$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL47', '  /map           the machines and their trunks$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL37', '  /topic         show the room topic$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', '  /topic <text>  set it$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL', '  /tell <name> <text>  a message to one person$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', '  /leave         leave the room$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL', '  /exit          Exit chat program$')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', '  anything else is said to the room$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /who - ask the room for its membership list.
        %
        % This used to print three lines saying the format had no kind for it.
        % It has one now: kWho = 11, added to ChatMessageKind.cs, CHATSV.PLNC
        % and here together, which is what those lines said the change needed.
        % The answer arrives later as an ordinary message and is printed by
        % showArrived - nothing waits here.
        % /who - who is in my room, or, WITH NO ROOM, who is anywhere.
        %
        % SENT EITHER WAY, and the same kind either way. This used to refuse
        % with "not in a room yet - wait or /join", which was the client
        % answering a question it is not the one that knows. The SERVER knows
        % whether we hold a seat, so it picks: kWho back if we do, kAllWho -
        % every room and everyone in it - if we do not.
        %
        % Ronny asked for exactly that on 2026-08-22, and it is the right shape
        % for the same reason /list is allowed unjoined: somebody with no room
        % is precisely the person who needs to be told where everybody is.
        %
        % THIS NEEDS serverMagic TO SURVIVE LEAVING, and it did not until the
        % same day. /leave used to zero it along with the joined flag, so this
        % question had no address to go to and printed nothing at all - the
        % server was answering correctly and the letter never left. The address
        % of the server and whether we hold a seat are two different facts; see
        % the note in the LEAVE branch.
        % ASKED BY HAND, so the answer is for this person and belongs in a
        % window rather than in everybody's room.
        IF cmdIs('WHO', 3) = 1 THEN
            TRUE =: askedWho
            buildOwn(kWho, 0) =: length
            sendToServer(length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /list - which rooms exist and how many are in each.
        %
        % ALLOWED WHILE UNJOINED, unlike /who. "What rooms are there" is exactly
        % the question somebody asks when they have just been refused a name and
        % are deciding where to go, and refusing to answer it then would be
        % refusing at the one moment it is useful. The server needs no seat to
        % answer - it walks the seats it already has.
        IF cmdIs('LIST', 4) = 1 THEN
            buildOwn(kRooms, 0) =: length
            sendToServer(length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /map - which machines there are and which trunks are up.
        %
        % ALLOWED WHILE UNJOINED, same as /list. "Is the other machine even
        % there?" is exactly the question somebody asks when the room looks
        % empty, and that is often when they have no seat either.
        %
        % READ-ONLY. There is deliberately no way to change a trunk from here -
        % START-TRUNK and STOP-TRUNK live in CHAT-MON, on the admin port, and
        % that separation is the whole reason /map is a room kind.
        IF cmdIs('MAP', 3) = 1 THEN
            buildOwn(kMap, 0) =: length
            sendToServer(length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /topic          show this room's topic
        % /topic <text>   set it, and tell everybody in the room
        %
        % Needs a room, because a topic belongs to one. The server refuses a
        % stranger in silence, so asking while unjoined would simply do nothing
        % at all - say why instead.
        IF cmdIs('TOPIC', 5) = 1 THEN
            IF NOT joined THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL34', 'not in a room yet - wait or /join$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % 7 = past "/TOPIC ". If the line is exactly "/TOPIC" there is no
            % space and typedLen is 6, so argLen comes out negative - hence
            % the "< 1" test rather than "= 0".
            7 =: at
            typedLen - at =: argLen
            IF argLen < 1 THEN
                % ASKING, not setting. An empty text is the question - and
                % remembering that we asked is the only way to tell OUR answer
                % from a topic somebody else changed, because both arrive as
                % the same kind.
                TRUE =: askedTopic
                buildOwn(kTopic, 0) =: length
                sendToServer(length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % Bounded to what the server will store, and said out loud rather
            % than quietly clipped - somebody who typed a long topic should not
            % have to work out for themselves where it was cut.
            IF argLen > maxTopicLen THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', 'that topic is too long (max 64)$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            buildOwnFrom(kTopic, at, argLen) =: length
            sendToServer(length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF cmdIs('JOIN', 4) = 1 THEN
            6 =: at
            typedLen - at =: argLen
            IF argLen < 1 THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', '/join needs a room name$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF argLen > maxNameLen THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'that room name is too long$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % LEAVE THE OLD ROOM FIRST. Without this the old room still counts
            % us as a member and still holds our seat: it never hears that we
            % went, and a room only gives a seat back on a clean leave or on a
            % reap. Two or three /joins and the room is full of people who are
            % not there. The reported "/join doesn't work" hid this one - the
            % join never reached a room, so the leak never had a chance to show.
            IF joined THEN
                buildOwn(kLeave, 0) =: length
                sendToServer(length)
            ENDIF

            % Re-point the room and send the join again. setRoom adds the
            % "CHAT-" prefix; the raw copy that stood here is the whole of the
            % /join defect.
            setRoom(at, argLen)
            FALSE =: joined
            0 =: serverMagic
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', 'joining ')
            showRoom
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL4', '...$')
            sendJoin
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF cmdIs('NICK', 4) = 1 THEN
            6 =: at
            typedLen - at =: argLen
            IF argLen < 1 THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL19', '/nick needs a name$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF argLen > maxNameLen THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'that name is too long$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            % NOT JOINED? THEN THIS IS NOT A RENAME - IT IS A RETRY.
            %
            % This used to answer "not in the room yet" and stop, which
            % left a refused user with NO WAY OUT: the server turns a
            % duplicate nickname away, and the client then refused the one
            % command that could fix it. The only escape was to quit and
            % edit CHAT:CNFG by hand, which no end user will do.
            % MEASURED 2026-08-20 with two terminals on one SINTRAN user:
            % both read the same CHAT:CNFG, both wanted the same name, and
            % the second was stuck.
            %
            % Both halves were individually right - the server should
            % refuse a duplicate, and a rename normally IS a message to a
            % room. The combination was the trap.
            %
            % With no room to tell, changing the name is purely local:
            % take the new name and knock again.
            % FROM HERE ON, ANY REFUSAL IS ABOUT A NAME THE USER PICKED.
            %
            % Set BEFORE the name is even validated, because the intent is what
            % matters: once somebody has typed /nick, "that name is taken" means
            % somebody else has it - NOT that they are in the room twice. See the
            % kReject handler, which switches its whole message on this.
            TRUE =: userChoseNick

            % DRAIN FIRST, THEN DECIDE. A WELCOME MAY BE SITTING UNREAD.
            %
            % MEASURED 2026-08-22 with the server running: typing /nick straight
            % after starting the client printed
            %
            %     joining (LOBBY) as SYSTEM
            %     trying TESTER
            %     you are TESTER  (/nick to change)
            %     cannot join as TESTER - already joined
            %
            % "trying" is printed by the retry branch BELOW, so that branch ran -
            % which means `joined` was still FALSE. It was false only because the
            % first join's kWelcome had not been PROCESSED yet; `joined` is set
            % where kWelcome is handled. So the client sent a SECOND join and the
            % server refused it, correctly, as already joined.
            %
            % Nothing was wrong with either branch. The state they switch on was
            % simply out of date, because the welcome was still in the port. That
            % is easy to hit here: the client only renders new lines when RETURN
            % is pressed, so a welcome can be waiting while the user is typing.
            %
            % One drain pass costs nothing and settles it. It is deliberately
            % BEFORE the NOT joined test rather than inside it - the same staleness
            % would make a rename look like a retry OR a retry look like a rename.
            drainPort

            IF NOT joined THEN
                setMyName(at, argLen)

                % "TRYING", NOT "YOU ARE" - WE ARE NOT THAT NAME YET. This is a
                % retry: the name goes into a fresh join and the SERVER decides.
                % Announcing it here and then printing the server's refusal two
                % lines later is what put
                %     you are ANNA
                %     OLAV: that nickname is taken
                % on the screen - the client claiming a name, the server denying
                % a different one, and nothing to connect the two.
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL7', 'trying ')
                showMyName
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                sendJoin
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % THE NEW NAME GOES IN THE NAME FIELD - see buildRename, which is
            % where the whole story of this being wrong the other way round is
            % written down. It reads the name straight out of typed, so there is
            % no shuffling to do here either.
            buildRename(at, argLen) =: length
            sendToServer(length)

            % Our own name changes only when the room says so, with kRenamed.
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /TELL NAME what to say - a message to ONE person.
        %
        % A SEAT IS REQUIRED, and not because the other person has to be in a
        % room - they do not, and that is half the point of the feature. It is
        % because the SENDER needs a name for the message to be from, which is
        % the same reason the server refuses this below its own seat test.
        IF cmdIs('TELL', 4) = 1 THEN
            IF NOT joined THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL34', 'not in a room yet - wait or /join$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % 6 = past "/TELL ". Exactly "/TELL" is typedLen 5, so argLen comes
            % out negative - hence "< 1" rather than "= 0".
            6 =: at
            typedLen - at =: argLen
            IF argLen < 1 THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL', '/tell NAME what to say - or D102!NAME$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % THE FIRST SPACE SEPARATES THE NAME FROM THE MESSAGE.
            0 - 1 =: cut
            FOR i IN 0:argLen - 1 DO
                IF cut < 0 THEN
                    IF typed(at + i) = spaceByte(0) THEN
                        i =: cut
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR

            IF cut < 1 THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'who to? /tell NAME what to say$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF argLen - cut - 1 < 1 THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'nothing to say to them$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % A SLASH IN THE NAME WOULD SPLIT IN THE WRONG PLACE at the far
            % end, and the message would go to a name nobody has. Said out
            % loud rather than sent and lost.
            FOR i IN 0:cut - 1 DO
                IF typed(at + i) = slashByte(0) THEN
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'a name cannot have a slash in it$')
                    RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR

            buildTell(at, cut, argLen - cut - 1) =: length
            sendToServer(length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF cmdIs('LEAVE', 5) = 1 THEN
            IF NOT joined THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL20', 'not in the room yet$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            buildOwn(kLeave, 0) =: length
            sendToServer(length)
            FALSE =: joined

            % THE MAGIC IS KEPT ON PURPOSE, and clearing it here was a bug.
            %
            % serverMagic is the ADDRESS OF THE SERVER. Leaving a room does not
            % make the server unreachable - it only means we no longer hold a
            % seat in it. Zeroing it left the client with nothing to send to, so
            % every question that is supposed to work while unjoined silently
            % did nothing:
            %
            %   /list  - which the wire registry has always said is allowed
            %            unjoined, and which is exactly what somebody asks when
            %            they have just left and are deciding where to go;
            %   /who   - the all-rooms answer built on 2026-08-22.
            %
            % MEASURED: after /leave, /who printed nothing at all. The server
            % was answering correctly; the question never left the building.
            %
            % joined is what gates SPEAKING, and it still does - see the main
            % loop. That flag and the address are two different facts and were
            % being cleared as if they were one.
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL14', 'left the room$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /exit IS THE NAME, /quit STILL WORKS.
        %
        % The help lists only /exit, so there is one answer to "how do I get
        % out" - but /quit has been in this client since it was written and in
        % the screen prototype beside it, and silently removing a word people
        % have in their fingers is a poor trade for tidiness. It costs one
        % test.
        %
        % Neither is a prefix of any other command, so /e and /q both work.
        IF cmdIs('EXIT', 4) = 1 OR cmdIs('QUIT', 4) = 1 THEN
            IF joined THEN
                buildOwn(kLeave, 0) =: length
                sendToServer(length)
            ENDIF
            FALSE =: running
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL4', 'bye$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        OUTPUT(1, 'AL28', 'no such command - try /help$')
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % The program.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % The prompt: WHO you are and WHERE you are, on the line you type into.
    %
    %     [OLAV]           before any room has been entered
    %     [OLAV/LOBBY]     once the server has welcomed us into one
    %
    % A prompt is not decoration here. Without one the program gives no sign it
    % is waiting, and with arriving lines landing on the same screen there was
    % nothing to tell a typed line from a received one.
    %
    % NOT YET: [OLAV/Private:ANNA] for a private channel with one other user.
    % There is no private channel in this client to be in - no /msg, no second
    % conversation - so a prompt claiming one would be inventing a state that
    % does not exist. It goes in when the thing it names does.
    %
    % showRoom is NOT used here: it wraps the name in parentheses for prose
    % like "joining (LOBBY)", and "[OLAV/(LOBBY)]" is one bracket too many.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showPrompt
        INTEGER : i

        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '[')
        showMyName
        IF joined THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '/')
            FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
                roomName(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', '] ')

        % PUT BACK WHAT WAS ALREADY TYPED.
        %
        % An arriving line is printed on the line being typed into - there is
        % one cursor and the room does not wait for anyone to finish a
        % sentence. Without this, a message arriving mid-sentence silently eats
        % half of it: the characters are still in the buffer and will still be
        % sent, but the screen shows an empty prompt, so the user retypes what
        % they had and sends it twice.
        FOR i IN 1:typedLen DO
            typed(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    PROGRAM : chatClient
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
        INTEGER : length
        INTEGER : i                   % the start-up room copy below uses it
        % How many bytes were still waiting when the keyboard was taken.
        % Screen mode only - see the drain beside the MON3 that turns the
        % echo off.
        INTEGER : drained

        INISTACK stack

        xmpfopn(0, myPort) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            showStatus('xmpfopn', returnStatus)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % A "port = N magic= M st= S" line used to print here, from a XMPFP2M
        % call kept only to feed it. It was there to answer one question: the
        % server was receiving the join, parsing it exactly right and answering
        % with status 0, and nothing was arriving back - so was the reply going
        % to the wrong address?
        %
        % IT WAS NOT. The reply address was never the bug. The two magic numbers
        % matched, which is what sent the search to the receive test in drainPort
        % where the real fault was: XMPFRCV returned XMOK, the test excluded
        % zero, and the welcome was dequeued and dropped. That is the whole
        % reason this diagnostic earned its keep, and the reason it can now go.
        %
        % XMPFP2M went with it. Nothing else read myMagic.

        % ===================================================================
        % HOW THIS CLIENT WATCHES TWO THINGS WITHOUT A NO-WAIT TERMINAL.
        %
        % The obvious design polls both: switch the terminal to no-wait with
        % TNOWAIT (307B) so INBT stops blocking, then spin on keyboard and port
        % together.
        %
        % ============================================================ SOLVED ==
        % EVERYTHING BELOW THIS LINE IS KEPT AS A RECORD OF A WRONG CONCLUSION.
        % It argued at length that a no-wait terminal could not be had here, and
        % that the client should therefore let the keyboard block. That decision
        % is what made an arriving line invisible until the user pressed return,
        % which on 2026-08-20 looked exactly like a broadcast reaching nobody
        % and cost an afternoon.
        %
        % THE WAY THROUGH IS MONITOR_CALL BY NAME, which the reasoning below
        % dismisses because "the MON-CALL-NAMES file was not found". That file
        % is supplied by MON-CALL-1B-A00, which is now INSTALLED and is already
        % in this client's LOAD list - CHATSV has been calling
        % MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) with it for weeks. And the call wanted is
        % not TNOWAIT (307B) at all but NoWaitSwitch (NOWT 36B), whose three
        % parameters are ALL INPUTS, so there is not even an output to get
        % wrong. See terminalNoWait and terminalWait above.
        %
        % The lesson is the one that keeps recurring on this project: a "cannot"
        % written down once outlives the thing that made it true. This note
        % survived the installation of the very package that answers it.
        % ======================================================================
        %
        % The old reasoning, for the record:
        %
        %   THE PORT IS DRAINED WITHOUT WAITING - xmpfrcv with flags ZERO.
        %   That is XMSG'S OWN no-wait and has nothing to do with the terminal:
        %   without XFWTF the call returns at once whether or not a message was
        %   there. So everything waiting can be taken and printed in a tight
        %   loop that never blocks.
        %
        %   THE KEYBOARD IS ALLOWED TO BLOCK - MON1, one character at a time,
        %   until carriage return.
        %
        % The one thing it costs: arriving lines are shown when you finish
        % yours, not the instant they land. Press RETURN on an empty line to
        % look. That is the whole price, and it buys a client that needs no
        % missing product, no assembler and no interface routine.
        %
        % Reading the keyboard needs INBT (1B). Getting a NO-WAIT terminal
        % would additionally need TNOWAIT (307B), and that is the one that
        % cannot be had:
        %
        % MONITOR_CALL IS THE WRONG TOOL, both ways round, and both were tried:
        %
        %   By NAME - Monitor_Call('InByte', ...), the form the Monitor Calls
        %   manual prints for PLANC - needs the MON-CALL-NAMES file. The
        %   compiler answers THE MON-CALL-NAMES FILE WAS NOT FOUND, and no such
        %   file exists under any of the nine users on this machine.
        %
        %   By NUMBER - MONITOR_CALL(1B, ...) - compiles and then does not
        %   link: it generates a call to 5MON_P, which is in neither
        %   PLANC-1BANK-F00 nor PLANC-2BANK-F00 (LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED after
        %   loading both).
        %
        % WHAT THE PLANC MANUAL ACTUALLY SAYS (ND-60.117.5, appendix 0.2,
        % "Monitor Calls Available on the ND-100 and the ND-500"): the runtime
        % provides monitor calls as ORDINARY ROUTINES, one per call, named MONn
        % and IMPORTed like anything else -
        %
        %     ROUTINE VOID, BYTE (INTEGER) : MON1 (dev)      % INBIT
        %
        % which is why the COSMOS guide's own sample opens with
        % IMPORT (ROUTINE VOID, VOID: MONO) - that is MON0, LEAVE. These are
        % the MN1, MN122, MN310 ... units sitting in PLANC-1BANK-F00. So INBT
        % is reachable, correctly, as MON1(1) - no MONITOR_CALL, no names file,
        % no 5MON_P.
        %
        % TNOWAIT IS NOT. The appendix lists MON 0,1,2,3,4,11,12,17,21,45,47,
        % 50,54,62,63,64,65,104,113,114,117,120,144,161,162,167,263 - and 307
        % is not among them. The manual says what to do about that in as many
        % words: "Any monitor calls not listed here, may be called from a PLANC
        % routine if a suitable interface routine is constructed by the user.
        % If this is done, the user must load the interface routine BEFORE the
        % PLANC runtime library."
        %
        % A hand-written interface routine for MON 307B, loaded ahead of
        % PLANC-1BANK-F00, is what the manual says to do about that.
        %
        % THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY THAT WAS IMPOSSIBLE HERE. It claimed "D100 has
        % neither MAC nor NPL installed (LIST-FILES found only MACM-AREA:DATA)".
        % BOTH HALVES ARE WRONG, and the way they were wrong is worth keeping.
        %
        % LIST-FILES IS THE WRONG COMMAND FOR THIS QUESTION. Compilers and other
        % subsystems are loaded into shared memory across users, not kept as :PROG
        % files in anybody's area, so LIST-FILES was never going to find one and
        % its silence proved nothing. The command that answers it is
        % LIST-REENTRANT. On D100, 2026-08-18:
        %
        %   START RESTART SEGMENT   NAME
        %  177777B 177776B    107B   MAC
        %      0B      1B    110B   NPL
        %    752B    752B    111B   PLANC-100
        %     0B      0B    112B   PED
        %    11B     11B    113B   FORTRAN-100
        %
        % MAC and NPL are BOTH there, alongside the PLANC that builds this program.
        % @MAC answers "- MAC -".
        %
        % So the door the manual describes is open: any monitor call outside the
        % runtime's ready-made MONn set can be reached with an interface routine
        % assembled in MAC and loaded BEFORE the PLANC library. TNOWAIT (307B) is
        % the one this program wanted, and with it the loop below could stop being
        % a busy one - see "what is not finished" at the foot of the file.
        %
        % The design below still stands on its own: it needs no interface routine,
        % and it works. But it is now a CHOICE rather than the only option, and
        % nobody should read this comment and conclude the machine cannot do it.
        % ===================================================================

        % SETTLE THE NAME BEFORE THE JOIN, because the join CARRIES it and
        % there is no second chance to put one in. Nothing is asked of the
        % user - see setupName.
        setupName

        % THE DEFAULT ROOM, BUILT BY THE SAME RULE AS A TYPED /join.
        %
        % This client joins a room at start-up whether or not the user asked
        % for one, and the bug report was right that nothing said so. It is
        % said now, with the name in parentheses.
        %
        % The short name goes through "typed" because setRoom reads from there.
        % That is deliberate: the prefix is then added in ONE place for both
        % paths, and the start-up name cannot drift from the /join name.
        FOR i IN 1:lenHomeRoom DO
            homeRoom(i - 1) =: typed(i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        setRoom(0, lenHomeRoom)

        % ---- WHICH RENDERER? Asked ONCE, here, and never again -----------
        %
        % blankscreen is VTM's first call and CTYTP is only valid AFTER it -
        % ask before and the bits are whatever was in memory. Two bits decide
        % it: the terminal is a VDU at all, and it can be told where to put
        % the cursor. Without both, a screen renderer would draw its frame as
        % a page of line noise on a printing terminal.
        %
        % ASKED ONCE because a renderer that could change under the program
        % would need every drawing site to cope with both, which is how a
        % screen ends up half painted.
        blankscreen
        MON16(1) =: termType
        TRUE =: screenOn
        IF (termType AND bitVdu) = 0 THEN
            FALSE =: screenOn
        ENDIF
        IF (termType AND bitCpos) = 0 THEN
            FALSE =: screenOn
        ENDIF

        IF screenOn THEN
            % TAKE THE KEYBOARD BEFORE A SINGLE THING IS DRAWN.
            %
            % No echo, because a raw key loop has no say in where the cursor
            % is and SINTRAN would drop each character wherever the last
            % drawing finished - inside the windows. MEASURED in CHATUI: with
            % this done after the first paint, every run came up showing
            % '> ??g??', and draining could not fix it because draining empties
            % the BUFFER and not the SCREEN it was already echoed to.
            %
            % And no ESC abort, which is what makes turning the echo off SAFE:
            % an abort would skip the exit path and hand back a terminal with
            % no echo at all.
            MON3(1, -1, echoTab)
            MON71(1)

            % WHATEVER IS ALREADY WAITING IS NOT THE USER TALKING TO THIS
            % PROGRAM - it is the tail of whatever was typed to get here, and
            % on a line whose terminal type was unset, the answer to VTM's own
            % question about it.
            DO
                MON66(1) =: drained
                WHILE drained > 0
                MON1(1) =: drained
            ENDDO
        ENDIF

        % WHAT IS BEING ATTEMPTED, not what has been achieved. The name here is
        % the one the join CARRIES; whether we get to keep it is the server's
        % answer, printed when the welcome arrives.
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', 'joining ')
        showRoom
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL4', ' as ')
        showMyName
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
        sendJoin

        % ONE LINE, NOT THE WHOLE COMMAND LIST. Printing all ten commands at
        % start-up buries the two lines that actually matter under a screen
        % nobody asked for.
        OUTPUT(1, 'AL37', 'Type /HELP for help and command list$')

        % showPrompt re-echoes whatever is in the typed buffer, so the buffer
        % has to MEAN something before the first prompt. Nothing above sets it,
        % and PLANC does not promise a module variable starts at zero.
        0 =: typedLen

        % THE FIRST PAINT GOES OVER THE JOIN BANNER, which is the point: those
        % lines were printed before the renderer was known and they are the
        % LINE renderer's, so on a screen they are simply painted over.
        IF screenOn THEN
            drawAll
            flush
        ELSE
            showPrompt
        ENDIF

        % FROM HERE THE TERMINAL IS IN NO-WAIT, so MON1 stops blocking and the
        % loop below can look at the port and the keyboard in turn. Switched on
        % AFTER the join is sent, so nothing above has to care, and switched off
        % again on the way out - see the end of the loop.
        %
        % ASK WHICH TERMINAL THIS IS FIRST. terminalNoWait needs the LDN and
        % there is no way to know it without asking - the number differs per
        % session and per machine, and the value the program used to pass was
        % not a terminal number at all.
        findMyLdn
        terminalNoWait

        % ONE LAST DRAIN, AND THIS ONE IS THE ONE THAT WORKS.
        %
        % There is already a drain up beside the MON3 that turns the echo off,
        % and it is not enough: the input line still came up holding '??g??'.
        % Those bytes arrive DURING the drawing, not before it - VTM asks the
        % line what terminal it is and the answer lands in the buffer while the
        % frame is being painted, so a drain that ran earlier could not have
        % caught them.
        %
        % Draining here, with the screen already up and immediately before the
        % first read, is the last moment anything can arrive unasked. typedLen
        % is cleared after it because a byte that slipped past the first drain
        % is already sitting in the buffer, and clearing without draining would
        % just let it back in on the next pass.
        IF screenOn THEN
            DO
                MON66(1) =: drained
                WHILE drained > 0
                MON1(1) =: drained
            ENDDO
            0 =: typedLen
            drawInput
            flush
        ENDIF

        DO
            % ---- take everything the port is holding, without waiting -------
            drainPort

            % ---- then take whatever HAS been typed, without waiting ---------
            % MON1 CAN RAISE A ROUTINEERROR, AND IT MUST BE CAUGHT HERE.
            %
            % Measured on D100: without this handler the client printed the
            % same arriving message for ever and ESC broke it out of a SPIN,
            % not out of a wait - so MON1 was not blocking at all. The terminal
            % is in no-wait BECAUSE terminalNoWait ABOVE PUT IT THERE, and that
            % call is new: for a long time this comment asserted the no-wait
            % state while nothing in the program ever asked for it, so MON1
            % really did block and the loop only came round when the user
            % pressed return. The Monitor Calls manual says what no-wait means:
            % "In No Wait, the program does not wait for input or output to
            % complete. Monitor calls like InByte return ERROR CODE 3 instead."
            % In PLANC that arrives as a ROUTINEERROR, and an unhandled one
            % unwinds the program - which re-entered, re-sent the join, and
            % produced another undeliverable letter coming back. One missing
            % handler, three symptoms.
            %
            % Caught, it is exactly the poll the design wanted: ErrCode 3 means
            % nothing was typed, so leave ch at zero and let the loop go round
            % to look at the port again. ErrCode is supplied by the runtime and
            % must NOT be declared.
            % lineReady stays FALSE until RETURN is seen. typedLen is NOT reset
            % here: a line half typed when the port needs draining has to
            % survive the trip round the outer loop, so it is only cleared once
            % the line has been dealt with.
            FALSE =: lineReady
            DO
                0 =: ch
                ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                    IF ErrCode >< emptyInput THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL21', 'terminal read failed$')
                    ENDIF
                    0 =: rawCh
                ENDON
                % READ THE SAME DEVICE THE SWITCH WAS SET ON.
                %
                % MEASURED ON D100 2026-08-22, and this is the second half of
                % the fix. Passing the real LDN to TerminalNoWait changed its
                % status from -1 to 0 - a genuine change, the call is now
                % accepted - and MON1 WENT ON BLOCKING ANYWAY. Everything was
                % still queued until a keystroke flushed it.
                %
                % Because the switch was set on the terminal's LDN while the
                % read was still asking for FILE 1. SINTRAN's "own terminal"
                % shortcut and the logical device are not the same object to
                % point a no-wait flag at, so the flag was on something this
                % read never touched.
                %
                % myLdn falls back to 1, so a machine where ExecutionInfo does
                % not answer reads exactly as before.
                %
                % SCREEN MODE ASKS MON66 FIRST AND ONLY THEN READS, and that is
                % not a tidier way of doing the same thing - it is the fix for a
                % client that joined and then went deaf.
                %
                % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26. With the echo turned off, MON1
                % BLOCKS whatever TerminalNoWait was told. The main loop sat in
                % the read, drainPort never ran, and the server's messages came
                % back undeliverable: SEATS said 1/16 - the client was properly
                % seated - while bounce climbed on every line sent to it. The
                % screen only ever changed when a key was pressed, because a
                % keystroke was the only thing that let the loop go round.
                %
                % Nothing on screen says "deaf". The seat count and the bounce
                % counter do, which is why they were the thing to look at.
                %
                % MON66 is ISIZE: how many bytes are WAITING. Ask that and MON1
                % is only ever called when there is certainly one to take, so it
                % cannot block. This is the same poll TESTUI and CHATUI use, and
                % the reason the no-wait route was abandoned there too.
                0 =: rawCh
                IF screenOn THEN
                    MON66(1) =: drained
                    IF drained > 0 THEN
                        MON1(myLdn) =: rawCh
                    ENDIF
                ELSE
                    MON1(myLdn) =: rawCh
                ENDIF
                % PARITY. Terminal input carries an even-parity bit in bit 7,
                % so carriage return arrives as 141, NOT 13. Compare without
                % masking and the client never sees the user press return and
                % looks hung rather than wrong.
                rawCh =: ch
                ch AND parityMask =: ch

                % ANY KEY CLOSES THE PANEL, AND DOES NOTHING ELSE.
                %
                % Consumed here, before every other test, so the key that
                % dismisses the window cannot also be typed into the input
                % line or read as a command. Anything that arrived while the
                % panel was up is in the cache already and appears the moment
                % the room is painted back.
                IF panelUp THEN
                    IF ch >< 0 THEN
                        FALSE =: panelUp
                        drawAll
                        flush
                        0 =: ch
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF

                IF ch = carriageRet THEN
                    TRUE =: lineReady

                    % A CARRIAGE RETURN IS NOT A NEW LINE ON THIS TERMINAL. It
                    % returns the cursor to column one of the line just typed
                    % and nothing moves it down, so whatever is printed next
                    % lands ON TOP of what the user wrote. That is what put
                    %     /whoIT-PROOF-LINE
                    % on the screen - "/who" with the next arriving line
                    % written over it - and it reads as the program mangling
                    % the input when in fact it simply never ended the line.
                    % One end-of-line here, the instant return is pressed.
                    %
                    % LINE MODE ONLY. On a screen there is no cursor wandering
                    % down the page - the input line is a fixed row that gets
                    % repainted, and an end-of-line written into it would scroll
                    % the whole frame up by one.
                    IF NOT screenOn THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF

                % Leave the moment the keyboard runs dry (ch = 0, nothing was
                % typed) or the line is finished. Going round again on an empty
                % buffer is what turns this into a spin instead of a poll.
                WHILE ch >< 0 AND NOT lineReady

                % BACKSPACE, and it only has to be handled in SCREEN mode.
                %
                % On a printing terminal SINTRAN's own echo does the erasing
                % before this program ever sees the line, which is why the test
                % below has always been "> 31" and nobody missed it. With the
                % echo turned off there is nobody else to do it.
                %
                % 127 as well as 8: VTM hands back 127 for the backspace key -
                % MEASURED with KEYPROB, where a sent 0x08 came back as 127.
                IF screenOn THEN
                    IF ch = 8 OR ch = 127 THEN
                        IF typedLen > 0 THEN
                            typedLen - 1 =: typedLen
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF

                IF ch > 31 AND ch < 127 THEN
                    % The bound. A full line stops accepting characters rather
                    % than writing past the end of typed - PLANC checks no array
                    % bound, so this test is the only thing between a held-down
                    % key and the variables laid out after it.
                    IF typedLen < maxTyped THEN
                        ch =: typed(typedLen)
                        typedLen + 1 =: typedLen
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF

                % SHOW WHAT HAS BEEN TYPED - but only once the burst is over.
                %
                % Repainting per character is not just slow, it is WRONG on a
                % paste: the repaint costs more than the terminal takes to
                % deliver the next byte, SINTRAN's 64-byte input buffer fills,
                % and the tail is dropped - and the tail is where the RETURN
                % is. Asking MON66 whether more is waiting is what tells us the
                % burst has not finished. MEASURED in CHATUI: 63 characters and
                % a RETURN arrived whole, 72 lost the RETURN.
                IF screenOn THEN
                    IF NOT lineReady THEN
                        MON66(1) =: drained
                        IF drained <= 0 THEN
                            drawInput
                            flush
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDDO

            IF lineReady AND typedLen > 0 THEN
                % NOTHING IS SENT BEFORE THE WELCOME. serverMagic is not known
                % until then, so sending here would post the line to address
                % zero. ChatClient.Say refuses the same way; this is that test.
                % A LEADING SLASH MAKES IT A COMMAND, not something to say.
                IF typed(0) = 47 THEN
                    handleCommand
                ELSE
                    IF joined THEN
                        buildOwn(kSay, typedLen) =: length
                        sendToServer(length)
                    ELSE
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL20', 'not in the room yet$')
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            % Cleared only once the finished line has been dealt with, so a
            % line still being typed is not thrown away by a drain pass.
            IF lineReady THEN
                0 =: typedLen
            ENDIF

            % PUT THE PROMPT BACK WHENEVER SOMETHING HAS BEEN WRITTEN OVER IT -
            % a command that has just been dealt with, or lines that arrived
            % from the room. Not on an idle pass, or an untouched screen would
            % fill with prompts a fifth of a second apart.
            % SOMETHING HAPPENED - repaint whichever renderer is in use.
            %
            % paintChat is the expensive one: 17 rows of 77 characters, about a
            % second and a half on a real 9600-baud line. It runs only when a
            % message actually arrived or a line was finished, never on an idle
            % pass, which is what keeps the loop cheap enough to also poll a
            % keyboard.
            IF lineReady OR lastDrainCount > 0 THEN
                IF screenOn THEN
                    % NOT WHILE A PANEL IS UP. Repainting the room would draw
                    % straight over the window the user is reading, and the
                    % lines are safe in the cache until they close it.
                    IF NOT panelUp THEN
                        IF lastDrainCount > 0 THEN
                            paintChat
                        ENDIF
                        drawStatus
                        drawInput
                        flush
                    ENDIF
                ELSE
                    showPrompt
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            % ---- NOTHING HAPPENED? THEN GIVE THE MACHINE A MOMENT -----------
            % With the terminal in no-wait, MON1 returns at once whether or not
            % anything was typed, so a pass that found no message AND no key is
            % a pass that would otherwise go straight round again at full speed.
            % That is a SPIN, and it is the same shape that burned the machine
            % under CHATSV when XMSG died - the note beside sleepUnits carries
            % the measurement.
            %
            % The sleep is skipped whenever ANYTHING happened, so a busy room
            % and a fast typist both stay immediate; only an idle client waits,
            % and then only a fifth of a second.
            IF lastDrainCount = 0 AND typedLen = 0 AND NOT lineReady THEN
                idleSleep
            ENDIF

            % /quit clears this. The continue-test goes LAST so the leave
            % message it sends is on its way before the loop ends.
            WHILE running
        ENDDO

        % PUT THE TERMINAL BACK. Anything that leaves this loop - /quit, or the
        % port going away when XMSG dies - hands the terminal back to the user's
        % shell, and it must be handed back in the state it was lent in. A
        % terminal left in no-wait is one every later program has to live with.
        terminalWait

        % AND ON A SCREEN, THREE MORE THINGS ARE OWED BACK.
        %
        % The echo, the escape key, and the terminal's own settings - in that
        % order, and BEFORE the screen is blanked, because blanking is what
        % clears the frame away and anything printed after it belongs to the
        % shell.
        %
        % LEAVING THE ECHO OFF IS THE WORST OF THE THREE. The user is returned
        % to a SINTRAN prompt that answers nothing they type, which reads as a
        % hung machine and is not one. resetscreen restores terminal STATE, not
        % content - blankscreen is what clears the picture.
        IF screenOn THEN
            MON3(1, 0, echoTab)
            MON72(1)
            blankscreen
            resetscreen
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

ENDMODULE

% NO $EOF HERE, deliberately, and it is not an oversight - an earlier version
% had one and it broke the build.
%
% $EOF belongs at the end of an INCLUDED source, where it stops the compiler
% reading past the file's end and calling that the end of the whole
% compilation. XMPIMP:INCL has one for exactly that reason, and so does the
% shipped (UTILITY)XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL.
%
% In the MAIN source it does something else: it ends the compiler SESSION. The
% compiler links and returns to SINTRAN there and then, so every command after
% the COMPILE - the EXIT, and any LOAD - is handed to SINTRAN instead, which
% answers ILLEGAL COMMAND and aborts the batch job. Measured on D100: the LOAD
% line arrived at the shell as "L".
%
% The library is brought in afterwards by BRF-LINKER-C01. See CHATCC:MODE.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% WHAT IS NOT FINISHED:
%
%  1. TWO ITEMS THAT USED TO BE ON THIS LIST WERE STALE, and they are recorded
%     here rather than quietly deleted, because a list of open problems that
%     keeps solved ones on it is worse than no list: it sends the next person
%     to work that is already done.
%
%       "Nothing has been compiled."   It compiles and it runs. See the header.
%
%       "No way to leave. kLeave is declared and never sent."   /leave and
%       /quit both send it - handleCommand, which was written after this line
%       and never came back to cross it off.
%
%     The remaining items below were re-read against the code on 2026-08-18 and
%     are still true.
%
%  2. THE LOOP IS A BUSY ONE. It reads the keyboard and the port as fast as the
%     machine will go, which on a shared SINTRAN is antisocial. The server does
%     not have this problem - it BLOCKS on XFWTF - but a client watching two
%     things cannot block on either one.
%
%     THE EXPERIMENT TO RUN, which is one specific thing rather than a research
%     project. Both halves have a documented wake:
%
%       the terminal   after a no-wait call, "SuspendProgram or WaitForRestart
%                      may passivate the program afterwards. The program
%                      restarts when input or output to the device is
%                      completed."          (Monitor Calls, NoWaitSwitch)
%
%       the port       XMPFRCV with XFWAK and WITHOUT XFWTF returns at once and
%                      ARMS a wake: "the next transmission to this port will
%                      lead to a wake up of the receiver task". Note that "when
%                      the wake up is done, the message is not received, and so
%                      the receiving must be repeated."   (COSMOS guide, XFWAK)
%
%     So the shape to try is: arm the port with XFWAK, then WaitForRestart, and
%     on waking re-issue the receive and re-read the keyboard, because either
%     could be what woke us.
%
%     AND THE OTHER ROUTE IS OPEN AFTER ALL: MAC and NPL are BOTH installed on
%     D100 (verified 2026-08-18 with LIST-REENTRANT, which is the command that
%     answers this - subsystems live in shared memory, not as :PROG files, so
%     LIST-FILES finding nothing proved nothing). So TNOWAIT (307B) can be reached
%     the way the PLANC manual says: an interface routine assembled in MAC and
%     loaded BEFORE PLANC-1BANK-F00. That is now a real option to weigh against the XFWAK
%     experiment below, not a closed door.
%
%     WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED is whether an XMSG wake-up actually restarts a
%     BACKGROUND PROGRAM passivated that way. The call that makes the wake-up
%     context explicit, XMPFWDF, is for DRIVERS - the guide says so, and adds
%     "not permitted for RT-programs". So this is one measurement, not a
%     deduction, and until it is made the loop stays as it is: honest and rude.
%     A short HOLD is the fallback if the wake does not arrive.
%
%  3. THE NAME IS NO LONGER FIXED AT RONNY, and this item is kept in corrected
%     form because it went through a wrong shape on the way.
%
%     It was briefly a PROMPT at start-up, and that was a mistake: on a machine
%     where you have already told SINTRAN who you are by logging in, being
%     asked your name again on every run is nagging. It is now read from
%     CHAT:CNFG in your own file area, changed with /nick, and saved only when
%     the ROOM confirms the rename - never when the request is sent, because
%     the room can refuse it.
%
%     WHAT IS STILL MISSING is the right fallback for somebody who has never
%     renamed: it should be the SINTRAN user name and it is still RONNY.
%     GetUserName is GUSNA 214B, and the PLANC runtime provides no MON214 -
%     the same gap CreateFile 221B falls into, which is why creating CHAT:CNFG
%     goes through MON70 instead. There is no command-level way round 214, so
%     it needs the route the PLANC manual describes in as many words: an
%     interface routine assembled in MAC and loaded BEFORE the PLANC runtime
%     library. MAC is installed here - LIST-REENTRANT shows it - so this is
%     work, not a wall.
%
%  4. The ON ROUTINEERROR form is taken from the Monitor Calls manual's own
%     PLANC example for InByte. That ErrCode 3 specifically means "nothing was
%     typed" is from the NoWaitSwitch entry, which says so for no-wait calls
%     generally rather than for InByte in particular. If some other code turns
%     up in practice, the handler above prints and carries on rather than
%     mistaking it for a keystroke.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
